const article1: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'how-much-do-driveway-gates-cost-kent-2026',
  title: 'How Much Do Driveway Gates Cost in Kent? 2026 Prices and Real Quotes',
  metaTitle: 'Driveway Gate Costs in Kent 2026 | Real Quotes and Guide Prices',
  metaDescription: 'What driveway and electric gates really cost in Kent in 2026: real South East quotes, guide prices by gate type, where the money goes, and how to tell if your quote is fair.',
  category: 'Pricing',
  publishDate: '2026-10-06',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-wrought-iron-open-manor-brick-pillars.png',
  excerpt: 'Real South East quotes for automated gates cluster between about £5,000 and £11,000, with sliding and hardwood gates often higher. Here is where that money goes and how to judge a quote.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: `Ask three Kent installers to price the same entrance and you can get three very different numbers. That is not always a sign that someone is overcharging. A driveway gate is part product and part building job, and the building part changes from one drive to the next. Real South East quotes, the national price guides and a line-by-line breakdown of a typical job show where the money goes and how to judge the figure in front of you.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What Homeowners in the South East Actually Paid' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Forum threads are the most honest source of prices, because people post what they were quoted and what they paid. On Mumsnet in March 2024, one homeowner shared quotes for a 1.2 metre high pair across a 3.5 metre opening: £3,200 for the gates alone, £4,500 for automated swing gates, and £7,200 for an automated sliding gate. Others in the same thread reported almost £10,000 including an intercom, around £11,000 including VAT for a 5 metre automated swing pair in galvanised and powder-coated steel, and £10,000 for sliding gates.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `On PistonHeads in March 2025, an owner with an 8 metre opening paid £10,500 including VAT for aluminium gates, posts, electrics, fitting, remotes and an intercom in the house, after quotes ranging from £9,000 to £16,500. One installer opened the conversation with a starting price of £9,000. Earlier in the same thread, a 6 metre sliding hardwood gate with automation was quoted at £13,000 twice. A poster who wanted a fully specified system from scratch put the ceiling at over £20,000.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Taken together, fitted automated pairs in the South East cluster between roughly £5,000 and £11,000, with sliding and hardwood gates often between £10,000 and £13,000, and large, fully specified entrances above that.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Why National Price Guides Run Lower' },
    { type: 'p', text: `National guides give lower figures. Checkatrade's electric gate installation page shows a range of £3,277 to £9,541 with an average of £5,712, and the same figures appear on its Maidstone, Canterbury and Sevenoaks pages, which tells you they are national rather than local. MyJobQuote, drawing on quotes made through its own platform, puts an average fitted job at around £3,000. Jacksons, the Kent gate manufacturer, says a single swing gate with ram automation starts at about £3,500 before VAT, with installation on top.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `The gap comes from what is included. Platform averages often cover a single gate on an easy site with power already nearby. The forum quotes above are for pairs, often with intercoms, new posts, trenching and full safety kit. A West Kent entrance with a long drive and brick piers is a different job from a single gate on a new-build close.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Guide Prices by Gate Type in Kent' },
    { type: 'p', text: `For a fully installed gate in Kent, these are the guide ranges we use. Automated sliding gates typically run from about £4,800 to £12,500, with cantilever systems, wide openings and premium materials at the top. Automated swing gates run from about £3,800 to £11,500, with underground motors and bespoke wrought iron at the higher end. Manual hardwood gates start at about £2,800. Adding motors to gates you already have usually costs £1,400 to £3,800, which is why [automating existing gates](/blog/electric-gate-automation-retrofit-kent/) is often the cheapest route to an electric entrance.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Where you live shifts the figure too. The commuter belt around [Sevenoaks](/location/sevenoaks/) and Tunbridge Wells sits at the top of these ranges, while North and East Kent are closer to the national guides. Coastal homes pay a little more for galvanised or marine-grade finishes.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Where the Money Goes in a Typical Quote' },
    { type: 'p', text: `A worked example from Trade2Base, a quoting tool used by installers, shows how a mid-range job adds up. For a 3.6 metre steel swing pair with ram motors, a keypad, a video intercom and a 12 metre armoured cable run, the total came to about £7,000. The gates were £1,600, the automation kit £1,300, photocells and safety edges £350, the keypad and intercom £700, posts and foundations £900, the electrician £750, and labour with force testing £1,400.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `The lesson is that the motor kit is a small share of the bill. PistonHeads posters put kit-only costs at roughly £800 to £1,700. Most of what you pay is groundwork, power, labour and the safety work that makes an automated gate legal to use.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What Pushes a Kent Quote Up' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Width and weight come first. A wider or heavier gate needs a stronger motor and deeper foundations, and [solid hardwood or wrought iron](/blog/best-gate-material-kent-wood-steel-aluminium/) weighs far more than aluminium. [Sliding gates usually cost more than swing gates](/blog/electric-sliding-vs-swing-gates-kent/) on the same opening; quotes in the forum threads above show a premium of roughly £2,000 to £2,700, mostly for the extra groundwork.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Power is the hidden cost. Trade2Base puts a new armoured supply from the consumer unit at £400 to £1,200, and a long rural drive can cost more to trench than the gate costs to make. Underground motors cost more than ram or arm motors and need drained chambers. In a conservation area or at a listed house, allow for drawings and an application; the national householder planning fee for gates rose to £272 from April 2026, according to the government's [planning fee schedule](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69a073bb07d7bff3604d6df6/Planning_fees_-_annual_indexation_from_1_April_2026.pdf).` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Is My Quote Fair?' },
    { type: 'p', text: `A fair quote is an itemised one. It should list the gate leaves with their material, size and weight; the posts or piers and their foundations; the motor brand and model; the safety devices, meaning photocells and safety edges; any access control; the electrical supply, trenching and reinstatement; the days of labour; force testing and a risk assessment; the handover paperwork; VAT shown separately; and the price of a service plan.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `If a quote is a single number with no breakdown, ask for one. If it is far below the others, find out what is missing: no safety edges, no force test, or no new supply are the usual gaps. Jacksons warns that if an installation is cheap, there could be a reason. If it is far above, ask what is driving it; underground motors, a long trench or bespoke fabrication can all be legitimate. [Choosing an installer](/blog/choosing-gate-installer-kent/) is as important as choosing the gate.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Running, Servicing and Repair Costs' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Running costs are small. Checkatrade estimates around £15 a year in electricity for a typical gate. Servicing costs more and matters more: [Gate Safe](https://www.gate-safe.org/how-often-should-automatic-gates-be-maintained-or-serviced/) recommends a service every six months by a trained installer, and price guides put a visit at roughly £100 to £300 depending on the system. Build that into the cost of ownership from the start.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Owners on PistonHeads describe the repair costs that catch people out. One replaced underground motors every two years at about £800 each until drain holes were drilled in the motor boxes; another paid £800 after a blocked soakaway flooded a chamber. Replacing the motors and controls on a 22-year-old system came to about £4,000. Good drainage and a regular [service and repair arrangement](/services/gate-repair-and-maintenance/) cost far less than repeated failures.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Getting Comparable Quotes' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Ask every installer to quote for the same specification: the same gate size and material, the same motor type, the same safety devices and the same access control. Ask each to visit, because a price given without a survey cannot account for your ground, slope or power supply. Then compare line by line. The cheapest total is rarely the cheapest gate to own, and a quote that includes force testing, proper drainage and a service plan will usually cost less over ten years than one that leaves them out.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'VAT, Deposits and Stage Payments' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Check whether each quote includes VAT. Some installers quote before VAT and some after, and on a £9,000 job the difference is £1,500, enough to make a dearer quote look cheaper. Ask for VAT shown as a separate line so you are comparing like with like.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Be careful with large deposits. A modest deposit to order bespoke gates is normal, but paying most of the price upfront leaves you exposed if the installer fails to finish. Stage payments tied to milestones, such as gates delivered, groundwork complete and gate commissioned, protect both sides. Keep the final payment until the gate has been force tested and you have the commissioning record in your hand.` },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};

const article2: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'electric-sliding-vs-swing-gates-kent',
  title: 'Sliding vs Swing Gates: Which Suits Your Kent Driveway?',
  metaTitle: 'Sliding vs Swing Driveway Gates | Which Suits Your Kent Drive?',
  metaDescription: 'Sliding or swing driveway gates? How to decide using your drive depth, run-back space, slope and budget, with real UK price differences and motor sizing explained.',
  category: 'Buying Guide',
  publishDate: '2026-10-06',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-aluminium-sliding-horizontal-modern-new-build.png',
  excerpt: 'The choice between a sliding and a swing gate usually comes down to three measurements: how deep your drive is, how much straight wall you have beside the opening, and how much the ground slopes.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: `Most people start with the look they want. Swing gates have a traditional, formal presence; sliding gates feel modern and tidy. But on a lot of Kent drives the ground makes the decision before taste does. Three measurements, the slope of the drive and a price gap of a couple of thousand pounds usually settle it.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'How Each Gate Opens' },
    { type: 'p', text: `A swing gate is hung on hinges and opens in an arc, either as a single leaf or a pair. Under section 153 of the [Highways Act 1980](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1980/66/section/153), a gate that opens onto a street must not open outwards, so a roadside swing gate always opens into your property. That means the arc of each leaf has to fit inside the drive, clear of the car.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `A sliding gate runs sideways along the boundary. A tracked sliding gate rolls on wheels in a steel track set across the opening. A cantilever sliding gate hangs from rollers on posts beside the opening and runs clear of the ground, with no track at all. Because a cantilever gate needs a counterbalance section, [Homebuilding and Renovating](https://www.homebuilding.co.uk/advice/electric-driveway-gates) notes that it must be about a third wider than the driveway itself.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Measure Your Drive Before You Choose' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Three measurements settle most decisions. The first is drive depth: for inward swing gates you need clear, level space behind the gate at least as long as each leaf, plus room for a car to stop clear of the arc. The second is run-back: a sliding gate needs a straight run of wall, fence or hedge line beside the opening at least as long as the gate, and longer for a cantilever. The third is width, which affects motor choice and cost; getting the [opening measured accurately](/blog/how-wide-should-driveway-gates-be-measuring-guide/) avoids a gate that leaves wide cars feeling tight.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Owners on the BuildHub forum describe both limits in practice. One found the opening width was capped by the length of straight wall the gate could slide behind; another widened a cantilever opening to 3.5 metres because wide cars felt cramped at the original size.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Short Drives and Busy Roads' },
    { type: 'p', text: `On a short drive, swing gates often fail the depth test. If the car cannot pull fully off the road before the gates swing open, it waits on the pavement or the carriageway, which is exactly what highways officers object to. A sliding gate needs no swing space, so it lets you set the gate closer to the road while keeping the car clear. That is why sliding gates are the usual answer on the new-build estates around [Dartford](/location/dartford/) and on the tight Victorian frontages of Canterbury.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Slopes and Rising Drives' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Jacksons, the Kent gate manufacturer, puts it plainly: upward slopes make swing gates unsuitable, and sliding gates are better suited to sloping ground. A swing leaf opening uphill catches the drive unless it is hung on [rising hinges](/blog/can-you-install-driveway-gates-on-a-sloped-driveway/), which lift the leaf as it opens, or cut to follow the slope. A cantilever sliding gate on a level concrete pad can float across a sloping drive without touching it.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `One BuildHub owner with an uphill drive was given a planning condition requiring inward-opening electric gates 5 metres back from the road, and the advice on the forum was rising hinges. The chalk scarp around [Maidstone](/location/maidstone/) and the Greensand ridge south of Westerham produce exactly these drives.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'The Real Price Difference' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Sliding gates usually cost more because they need more groundwork: a track foundation or a cantilever pad, plus the posts and rollers. Real quotes show the gap. On Mumsnet in March 2024, a homeowner was quoted £4,500 for an automated swing pair and £7,200 for an automated sliding gate on the same 3.5 metre opening. On PistonHeads in 2020, an installer quote suggested cutting about £2,000 by choosing opening gates over sliding, because the sliding mechanism adds cost. Expect a [sliding premium of roughly £2,000 to £2,700](/blog/how-much-do-driveway-gates-cost-kent-2026/) on a comparable job.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Motors Are Sized Differently' },
    { type: 'p', text: `The two types are motorised in different ways. Nice, the motor manufacturer, sizes sliding motors by gate weight, with tiers up to 400 kilograms, up to 600 kilograms and above. Swing motors are sized by leaf length, with tiers up to 3.5 metres, 4.5 metres and 7 metres. So a heavy hardwood sliding gate can push you into a bigger motor, while a long swing leaf does the same for a swing pair. Your installer should weigh and measure the gate before choosing the motor, and an [automated system](/services/automated-gate-systems/) quote should name the model.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Safety Points for Each Type' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Both types can trap and crush if they are not set up properly. Gate Safe, the UK safety charity, was founded in 2010 after two children were killed by automated gates. Swing gates create crush points at the hinge side and the closing edge; sliding gates create them where the gate meets the end post and where it runs past the wall. Both need photocells, safety edges and force testing, and an open-bar sliding gate needs protection so a hand cannot reach through into the moving parts.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Wind and Solid Gates' },
    { type: 'p', text: `A solid, close-boarded leaf catches the wind. On a swing gate that load acts on the hinges and the motor every time a gust hits, and on an exposed coastal or hilltop plot a solid swing gate can strain an undersized motor. A sliding gate presents the same face to the wind but moves along its length rather than swinging into it. On the most exposed plots, open designs with spaced bars or slats let the wind through whichever type you choose.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Which Suits Which Kent Property' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Large period houses in Sevenoaks, Tunbridge Wells and the West Kent villages, with deep drives and brick piers, suit a pair of swing gates with underground motors. New-build estates in North Kent and around Ashford usually suit an aluminium sliding gate. Tight town frontages in Canterbury, Faversham and Rochester often have room only for a single sliding leaf. Farmhouses and barns in the Weald suit timber swing gates, often a five-bar style. Sloping drives under the Downs or on the Greensand ridge suit a cantilever slider or swing gates on rising hinges.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `If you are still undecided, ask each installer to quote for both on the same survey. The comparison makes the trade-off concrete: the extra cost of sliding against the space and slope problems it solves.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `When the installer visits, walk the drive together with the car. Open the car doors where you would normally park, check where a swing leaf would stop, and pace out the straight run beside the opening. Ten minutes on site with a tape measure usually answers the question more reliably than any brochure, and it shows you which installer has thought about how you actually use the drive.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Pedestrian Access With Each Type' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Opening a full driveway gate every time someone walks in wastes time and wears the motor. Most installers recommend a separate [pedestrian gate alongside the driveway gates](/blog/pedestrian-gate-alongside-driveway-gates-kent/), and the gate type affects where it can go. With swing gates, the pedestrian gate usually sits to one side on its own post. With a sliding gate, it cannot sit in the path of the run-back, so it goes on the opposite side of the opening or in a separate section of wall.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Some sliding systems offer a pedestrian mode that opens the gate only partly, enough for a person but not a car. It is a useful compromise where there is no room for a separate gate, but it still runs the motor, and it does not help in a power cut. A manual pedestrian gate with its own lock is simpler and lets you leave the main gate closed.` },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};

const article3: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'planning-permission-driveway-gates-kent',
  title: 'Do You Need Planning Permission for Driveway Gates in Kent?',
  metaTitle: 'Planning Permission for Driveway Gates in Kent | 2026 Rules',
  metaDescription: 'When driveway gates in Kent need planning permission: the 1m and 2m rule, listed buildings, conservation areas, Article 4 directions, National Landscapes, new accesses and outward-opening gates.',
  category: 'Planning',
  publishDate: '2026-10-06',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-wrought-iron-open-stone-pillars-lanterns-estate.png',
  excerpt: 'Most driveway gates are permitted development up to 1 metre beside a road and 2 metres elsewhere. Listed buildings, Article 4 directions and new accesses are where Kent homeowners need permission.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: `Most driveway gates in Kent do not need planning permission. The exceptions are specific, and they catch out a lot of homeowners: a listed house, an Article 4 direction, a new access onto a main road, or a gate that is simply too tall. Electric operation is not one of them. The national rules come first, then the Kent layers on top.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'The Permitted Development Rule' },
    { type: 'p', text: `In England, gates, fences and walls are covered by Class A of Part 2 of the [General Permitted Development Order 2015](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/596/schedule/2/part/2). It allows a new gate up to 1 metre high where it is next to a highway used by vehicles, and up to 2 metres high elsewhere. A replacement or alteration may be as tall as the old one or as tall as these limits, whichever is greater, so an existing tall gate can usually be replaced like for like.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `That is the whole height rule. A 1.8 metre electric gate right on the pavement edge needs planning permission; the same gate set well back inside a front garden, away from the road, may not count as adjacent to the highway. Where a gate sits on the line between the two, ask the council for a view before you order.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Listed Buildings and Their Curtilage' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Class A does not apply within the curtilage of a listed building, or to any gate, fence or wall surrounding one. A gate at a listed house always needs planning permission, and if the work touches the listed structure, such as a historic wall or pier, it needs [listed building consent](/blog/listed-building-consent-driveway-gates-kent/) as well. Kent has dense clusters of listed houses in Canterbury, Faversham, Sandwich, Rochester and Tenterden, so this is the most common reason a Kent gate needs consent.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Conservation Areas: The Height Limit Is the Same' },
    { type: 'p', text: `A common myth is that a conservation area removes the right to put up a gate. It does not. Class A has no exclusion for conservation areas, so the 1 metre and 2 metre limits apply there exactly as they do elsewhere. The catch is demolition. Taking down an existing gate or wall in a conservation area can count as relevant demolition and need permission, so replacing an old wall with a new gate can bring the whole job into planning.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Councils also expect a design that suits the area, and they will judge any application against that. A painted metal gate matched to the street, or timber in a village, is far more likely to be approved than a tall solid gate across an old frontage.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Article 4 Directions Across Kent' },
    { type: 'p', text: `An Article 4 direction removes permitted development rights in a defined area, and many Kent councils use them on front boundaries. Where one applies, even a low gate needs planning permission. [Gravesham has thirteen conservation areas](/location/gravesend/) where building or altering gates, walls and fences needs consent. Swale's direction covers the whole [Faversham](/location/faversham/) Conservation Area. Dover District has directions on gates in Deal's Middle Street, Sandwich's walled town and Dover's Dour Street. Medway controls boundary gates on named streets in Rochester, Chatham and Gillingham, and Sevenoaks District has brought one forward for Kemsing.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `[Ashford](/location/ashford/) is unusual in having several directions aimed specifically at gates and fences rather than whole areas. The [Planning Portal](https://www.planningportal.co.uk/permission/common-projects/fences-gates-and-garden-walls/planning-permission) lists Article 4 directions among the conditions that can remove the right, and the only reliable way to know is to ask your district council, because not every council publishes its directions to the national register.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Kent Downs and High Weald National Landscapes' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty adopted the name National Landscapes on 22 November 2023, although the legal term is unchanged. The Kent Downs and the High Weald cover large parts of the county. As with conservation areas, the designation does not remove the 1 metre and 2 metre rights for gates. What it changes is the judgement on any application that is needed: councils in these areas expect gates that suit a rural setting, usually timber or simple traditional metal, and are wary of tall solid gates, oversized piers and suburban-looking entrances on country lanes.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Green Belt and New Entrances' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Green Belt does not change the permitted development heights either. It matters when you form a new entrance or put up gates that need permission anyway, because the council then weighs the effect on openness. In 2021 Sevenoaks District Council refused fencing and entrance gates at Knockholt as inappropriate development in the Green Belt, while it approved new gates with pillars at [Farley Grange near Westerham](/location/westerham/) in 2026. Keep gates no taller than needed, choose open designs and keep hedges either side.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'New Accesses and Dropped Kerbs' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Class B of the same Part allows a new access to a highway only where the road is not a trunk road or a classified road. A new entrance onto an A, B or C road therefore needs planning permission. Kent County Council will only process a dropped kerb on a classified road once you have planning permission, or written confirmation from the district that none is needed. In Medway, the unitary council handles both planning and the dropped kerb itself.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Gates Must Not Open Outwards' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Separately from planning, section 153 of the Highways Act 1980 says a gate that opens onto a street must not open outwards. The highway authority can serve notice requiring it to be altered, give the owner eight days to comply, and then do the work itself and recover the cost. This is why every roadside driveway gate in Kent either swings inwards or slides.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Electric Operation Is Not the Trigger' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Adding motors to a gate does not by itself need planning permission. The planning questions are height, listed status, Article 4 directions and the access. Automated gates do have their own legal duties, under safety law rather than planning law, so make sure your installer [commissions an automated gate](/services/automated-gate-systems/) properly, with force testing and a Declaration of Conformity at handover.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'How to Apply and What It Costs' },
    { type: 'p', text: `If you need permission, the application goes to your district council, or Medway Council in Medway. You will need a location plan, drawings of the gate and piers, and a short description of materials and colour. The national householder fee for gates, fences and walls is £272 from April 2026. Many installers who work in conservation areas will prepare the drawings for you, so ask whether that is included in the quote, and do not let fabrication start until consent is in place.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'If a Gate Goes Up Without Permission' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Building a gate that needed permission without getting it is not a criminal offence in itself, but the council can take enforcement action and require the gate to be altered or removed. The usual route out is a retrospective planning application, and Kent councils see these regularly: applications for walls, railings and gates have been made after the work at addresses in Folkestone and Challock, among others. A retrospective application is judged on the same merits as any other, so a gate that would have been refused beforehand can still be refused afterwards.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Unauthorised work also causes trouble when you sell. A buyer's solicitor may ask for evidence that the gate was permitted, and an unresolved breach can delay the sale. If you are unsure whether your gate needed consent, ask the council for a lawful development certificate, which confirms in writing that a project is permitted development.` },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};

const article4: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'best-gate-material-kent-wood-steel-aluminium',
  title: 'Best Driveway Gate Material for Kent: Wood, Steel, Aluminium or Iron',
  metaTitle: 'Best Driveway Gate Material | Wood vs Steel vs Aluminium (Kent)',
  metaDescription: 'Wood, steel, aluminium, wrought iron or composite? How each driveway gate material lasts, what it weighs, what it costs and which suits coastal and inland Kent homes.',
  category: 'Materials',
  publishDate: '2026-10-06',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-wooden-painted-cream-kentish-countryside.png',
  excerpt: 'The right gate material depends on where you live, how heavy a gate your motor can move, and how much maintenance you will actually do. Here is how wood, steel, aluminium, iron and composite compare.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: `Material is the decision that shapes everything else about a gate: how it looks, how long it lasts, how much maintenance it needs and how big a motor it requires. In Kent there is an extra factor, because a gate a few streets from the sea in Whitstable or Deal faces very different weather from one in a Weald village. Each material wins somewhere, and the old question of wooden against metal has a clearer answer once weight and location are on the table.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Timber: Oak, Iroko and Accoya' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Hardwood remains the natural choice for oast houses, barn conversions and village lanes. Iroko is very durable and resistant to rot and insect attack, which is why it is sometimes used as a substitute for teak, according to the [Wood Database](https://www.wood-database.com/iroko/). European oak also has very good resistance to decay and weathers to a silver grey if left untreated. Both are heavy: dried iroko weighs around 660 kilograms per cubic metre and oak around 675.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Accoya, a modified radiata pine, is marketed for exposed positions. Its manufacturer offers a 50-year warranty above ground and claims a 60-year service life and much less swelling than traditional timbers. Those are manufacturer claims rather than independent tests, but they explain why Accoya is often specified for coastal gates. Kent manufacturer Jacksons guarantees its treated softwood for 25 years against rot and insect attack, which sets a useful benchmark for cheaper timber.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Timber needs looking after. Expect to [oil a hardwood gate regularly](/blog/how-to-look-after-wooden-driveway-gates/) and check the hinges, and use stainless fixings, because joiners commonly warn that oak reacts with ordinary steel and stains black.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Steel: Galvanised and Powder Coated' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Steel is the most common material for automated gates, according to installer quoting tool Trade2Base, because it is strong, workable and affordable. Its weakness is rust, and the finish decides how long it lasts. Hot-dip galvanising coats the steel in zinc, which corrodes slowly in place of the steel. The [Galvanizers Association](https://www.galvanizing.org.uk/) shows how much location matters: an 85 micron coating lasts an average of 170 years where zinc corrodes at 0.5 microns a year, but only 34 years at 2.5 microns a year, and its postcode map lets you check your own site.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `A powder coat over the galvanising gives the colour and adds a second barrier. Jacksons gives its galvanised steel railings a 25-year service life guarantee against rust. On the coast, ask for both layers, with a [marine-grade powder coat](/blog/coastal-gate-corrosion-protection/) over the zinc.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Aluminium' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Aluminium does not rust and is much lighter than steel, which Trade2Base describes as kind to the motor. A lighter gate can use a smaller motor and puts less load on posts and hinges, which matters on sloping or exposed plots. It costs more than steel for the same design, and it does not take the ornate detailing of forged iron, but for modern houses and seafront homes in Thanet, Herne Bay or Folkestone it is often the best long-term value.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Wrought Iron' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Ornate iron gates suit large period houses in Sevenoaks, Tunbridge Wells and the West Kent villages. They are very heavy, and Trade2Base notes they need the strongest motors and the best foundations. That weight is the hidden cost: it can push a gate into a more expensive motor tier and demands deep footings for the piers. Galvanising before painting is just as important on iron as on steel.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Composite' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Composite gates use plastic-and-wood-fibre slats on an aluminium frame, sold on the promise of never needing paint. They suit modern homes and owners who want the look of boarding without the oiling. They are newer to the market than timber or steel, so long-term performance in UK weather is less proven, and warranty terms vary between makers.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Weight Decides the Motor' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Motor makers size their products by weight and length. Nice, for example, uses tiers for sliding gates up to 400 kilograms, up to 600 kilograms and above, and for swing gates by leaf length up to 3.5, 4.5 and 7 metres. A fully boarded hardwood or iron gate can move you up a tier, which costs more and can mean a larger, more visible motor. If you want a heavy material on an [automated gate](/services/automated-gate-systems/), make sure the motor is chosen after the gate is designed, not before.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What Each Material Costs' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Trade2Base gives these installed price bands for a pair of leaves, before automation: timber £600 to £2,500, galvanised and powder-coated steel £800 to £3,500, aluminium £1,200 to £4,000, and wrought iron £1,500 to £6,000 or more. MyJobQuote's platform data shows wooden and metal electric gates of the same size pricing within the same bands, so for a simple design the material choice changes the price less than people expect. Bespoke ironwork and wide hardwood gates are where costs climb, and the gate itself is only part of a [full installed quote](/blog/how-much-do-driveway-gates-cost-kent-2026/).` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Wooden or Metal Gates?' },
    { type: 'p', text: `For most homeowners the real choice is wood against metal. Wood looks warm and traditional, suits rural and period settings, and is what planning officers usually expect in conservation areas and National Landscapes. It needs regular maintenance, moves with the weather and is heavy. Metal suits town houses and modern homes, needs little maintenance if properly galvanised, and comes in lighter options. Owner experience backs up spending on quality either way: one Mumsnet poster said cheap gates they fitted themselves looked rubbish after five years, while their custom gates still opened with fingertip pressure three years on.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Which Material for Which Part of Kent' },
    { type: 'p', text: `On the coast, from [Whitstable](/location/whitstable/) round to Folkestone, aluminium or galvanised steel with stainless fixings lasts longest. In the Weald, around Cranbrook and Tenterden, oak or iroko suits the farmhouses and oasts. In the West Kent commuter belt, painted iron or steel on brick piers suits the period houses. On new-build estates across North Kent and Ashford, aluminium or powder-coated steel keeps maintenance low. Whatever you choose, ask the installer how the gate will be braced, how heavy it will be and which motor it needs.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Finishes and Colours' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Metal gates are powder coated in a RAL colour, and the colour matters more than people expect. Black and dark greens are the usual choice for period houses and conservation areas, where councils tend to favour colours that recede. Anthracite grey suits modern homes. Light colours show dirt and, on the coast, any rust bleeding from a damaged edge. Ask for a colour sample on the actual finish before you approve the order.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Timber can be left to weather, oiled to keep its colour, or painted. Painted timber, often white or cream on weatherboarded houses in the Weald, needs repainting every few years, while oiled hardwood needs a fresh coat roughly once a year. Unfinished oak or iroko will turn silver grey and still last well.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'How Long Each Material Lasts' },
    { type: 'p', text: `With reasonable care, all of these materials will outlast the motor, which usually needs replacing first. Well-maintained hardwood lasts for decades, and Accoya carries a 50-year warranty above ground. Galvanised and powder-coated steel lasts for decades inland, with the Galvanizers Association figures showing how much the coast shortens that. Aluminium is the least affected by weather of all. The weak points are almost always joints, fixings and finishes rather than the material itself, so quality of fabrication matters as much as the material you choose.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `When comparing quotes, ask each installer what the gate will weigh, how the leaves are braced, what finish or treatment is included, and what the warranty covers on the gate as opposed to the motor. A clear answer on all four is a good sign that the gate has been designed for your entrance rather than picked from a catalogue.` },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};

const article5: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'electric-gate-automation-retrofit-kent',
  title: 'Adding Electric Automation to Existing Gates in Kent',
  metaTitle: 'Gate Automation Retrofit Kent | Add Electric Operation to Manual Gates',
  metaDescription: 'Retrofit automation to your existing Kent driveway gates. This guide covers feasibility, motor options, access control, and what the process involves from survey to commissioning.',
  category: 'Automation',
  publishDate: '2026-02-01',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-underground-motor-installation-engineer.png',
  excerpt: 'Existing manual gates that are structurally sound can almost always be automated. The retrofit adds electric operation without disturbing the gates themselves.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'Gate automation retrofits are among the most frequently requested projects for installers covering Kent. The typical brief is straightforward: a homeowner with manual gates that are the right look for the property, still in sound condition, but no longer acceptable as a daily manual task. The retrofit adds the [motor, safety systems, and access control](/services/automated-gate-systems/) without replacing the gates.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Feasibility: What Gets Checked at the Survey' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The installer assesses gate weight, hinge condition, post alignment, foundation adequacy, and available power supply. Common issues that need resolving before automation include sagging hinges, posts that have shifted, and gates that are heavier than the proposed motor can handle. All are straightforward to address. The one situation where a retrofit is not cost-effective is where the gate itself needs replacing, in which case a new gate and motor package makes better financial sense.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Underground vs Ram-Arm Motors' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Underground motors sit in a chamber below the post and are invisible when the gate is closed. They are the standard choice on premium West Kent properties in the Sevenoaks and Tunbridge Wells belt where the appearance of the entrance matters. [Ram-arm motors](/blog/best-electric-gate-motor-brands-uk-compared/) mount on the face of the gate and post, visible from inside the property. They are the practical choice on retrofit projects where the post foundations do not support excavation, and are widely used across the North Kent and Maidstone areas.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Access Control Options' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Beyond basic remote operation, a retrofit can include video intercom with smartphone access, proximity readers that open the gate as your vehicle approaches, keypad entry, and integration with smart home platforms. For Kent homeowners in the Sevenoaks and Tunbridge Wells belt where high-value vehicle ownership is concentrated, a video intercom with recording capability adds a genuine security dimension to the automation investment.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What the Retrofit Costs in Kent' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A quality automation retrofit in Kent costs £1,400 to £3,800 depending on gate weight, motor type, and access control specification. Underground motors sit at the higher end of the range. The installation takes 1 to 2 days from arrival on site. BS EN 12453 safety commissioning and a written handover are included as standard on every retrofit in our Kent network.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};

const article6: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'choosing-gate-installer-kent',
  title: 'How to Choose a Driveway Gate Installer in Kent',
  metaTitle: 'How to Choose a Gate Installer Kent | What to Check in 2026',
  metaDescription: 'Not all gate installers in Kent are equal. This guide explains what to check, what questions to ask, and what separates a specialist from a generalist.',
  category: 'Buying Guide',
  publishDate: '2026-02-05',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-steel-installation-two-engineers-suburban.png',
  excerpt: 'The quality of the installation matters more than the brand of gate or motor. Here is how to identify a Kent gate installer who will get the job right first time.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'A gate installer who specialises in residential gate work and has a documented track record of completed projects in Kent is a fundamentally different proposition from a general builder who occasionally takes on gate jobs. The difference shows in the foundation specification, the motor sizing, the safety commissioning, and the quality of the handover documentation. It also shows three years later when [something needs attention](/blog/electric-gate-maintenance-kent/) and the installer is still in business and still answering the phone.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Check the Track Record' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Ask for evidence of completed residential gate installations. A genuine specialist will have a portfolio of finished projects, ideally including work in your part of Kent. Look for experience with your specific gate type: a firm that primarily installs aluminium sliding gates on new-build estates may not be the right fit for a bespoke hardwood installation in the High Weald National Landscape.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Verify Insurance and Warranties' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Public liability insurance of at least £2 million is the baseline for any gate installer working on residential properties. Warranties should be provided separately for the gate structure and the automation system, not lumped together in a vague catch-all. Ask for the warranty terms in writing before signing a contract.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Kent-Specific Planning Knowledge' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Kent has two National Landscapes, Green Belt through the Sevenoaks and Dartford areas, and conservation areas in every historic town. An installer who knows the county will identify planning considerations at the survey stage before they become problems. They will know which of the 13 councils covers your property and what the local policy says about boundary treatments. This knowledge is a genuine differentiator that matters more in Kent than in counties with simpler planning landscapes.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'BS EN 12453 Commissioning' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Every [automated gate in the UK](/services/automated-gate-systems/) should be commissioned to BS EN 12453 with force testing and documented safety checks at handover. Ask any installer whether they carry a force measurement device and whether they will provide a written commissioning record. An installer who cannot confirm both of these should not be considered for any automation project.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Coastal Specification Knowledge' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If your property is in East Kent near the coast, the installer should know the higher-specification surface treatments required for salt air exposure. Marine-grade powder coat, aluminium over steel where appropriate, and stainless steel fixings rather than standard zinc-plated are all relevant. An installer who does not raise these points for a coastal Kent property may not have the experience to get the specification right.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};

const article7: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'electric-gate-maintenance-kent',
  title: 'Electric Gate Maintenance in Kent: What Your Annual Service Should Cover',
  metaTitle: 'Electric Gate Maintenance Kent | Annual Service Guide 2026',
  metaDescription: 'Annual servicing prevents the majority of electric gate failures. This guide covers what a proper Kent gate service includes, what it costs, and why skipping it is a false economy.',
  category: 'Maintenance',
  publishDate: '2026-02-08',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-underground-motor-installation-engineer.png',
  excerpt: 'A proper annual service covers motor, gearbox, hinges, safety sensors, battery backup, and the gate structure. Here is what to expect and what it should cost in Kent.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'An automated gate system is a piece of machinery that operates outdoors through every season. It needs [annual professional servicing](/services/gate-repair-and-maintenance/) for the same reason a boiler or a vehicle does: mechanical components wear, lubricants break down, electronic calibrations drift, and the safety systems that protect your family need to be tested and confirmed working at regular intervals.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What a Full Annual Service Covers' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Motor and gearbox lubrication. Drive mechanism inspection: rack-and-pinion on sliding gates, arm or underground mechanism on swing gates. Hinge condition assessment and torque check. Safety sensor alignment and sensitivity testing for photocells and safety edges. Battery backup charge level and load test. Intercom and access control function verification. Track cleaning and roller inspection on sliding gates. Visual inspection of the gate structure, posts, finish, and fixings.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What It Costs in Kent' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Annual servicing in Kent typically costs £120 to £210 for a standard residential gate system. The variation reflects travel distance, system complexity, and whether the system includes intercom or advanced access control that needs testing. This cost is consistently less than a single emergency callout and repair, and it keeps the manufacturer warranty valid on systems still within the warranty period.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Coastal Kent: Additional Considerations' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Properties near the East Kent coast between Whitstable and Folkestone face accelerated corrosion on steel and iron components due to salt-laden air. An annual service on a coastal installation should include specific inspection of the finish condition, checking for any chips or scratches that could allow salt corrosion to start, and application of protective treatment to any exposed areas. Stainless steel fixings should be checked for the early signs of crevice corrosion that can occur even on marine-grade stainless in high-salt environments.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Why Skipping a Service Is a False Economy' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The [most common gate failures](/blog/what-to-do-when-electric-gates-stop-working/) seen by Kent engineers are preventable with annual servicing. A motor that fails from lack of lubrication costs £350 to £700 to replace. A safety sensor that drifts out of alignment and is not caught until it causes repeated false stops generates a callout fee. A battery backup that dies silently leaves the gate stuck open during the next power cut. Annual servicing catches all of these at the point where they are cheap to address.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};

const article10: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'driveway-gates-home-security-kent',
  title: 'Driveway Gates and Home Security in Kent: What Actually Works',
  metaTitle: 'Driveway Gates Security Kent | Vehicle Theft Protection 2026',
  metaDescription: 'How effective are driveway gates against vehicle theft and burglary in Kent? This guide covers what works, what does not, and how to specify a gate for security.',
  category: 'Security',
  publishDate: '2026-02-18',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-video-intercom-panel-brick-wall-closeup.png',
  excerpt: 'A closed automated gate is a proven deterrent against driveway vehicle theft. Here is how to specify your Kent gate installation with security as a priority.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'Vehicle theft from driveways, primarily through relay attacks on keyless entry systems, is a documented concern across the wealthier parts of Kent. The Sevenoaks and Tunbridge Wells corridor, with its concentration of high-value vehicles on residential driveways, is a consistent target. A closed automated driveway gate is one of the most effective physical deterrents available, not because it is impossible to breach, but because it makes the property a significantly harder target than a neighbouring property without one.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Why Gates Deter Vehicle Theft' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Relay theft relies on speed. The criminals need to approach the house closely enough to amplify the key fob signal, walk to the vehicle, start it, and drive away. A closed gate between the road and the vehicle forces a second obstacle into this chain: the gate itself must be passed before the vehicle can be reached, and the vehicle cannot be driven through a closed gate. The additional time, noise, and visibility involved in breaching or climbing a gate is often enough to redirect the attempt to an easier target.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Auto-Close and Timed Locking' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Automatic closing after a set delay is a standard feature on all automated gate systems and should be enabled for any installation where security is a primary consideration. Most control boards allow the auto-close delay to be set between fifteen seconds and several minutes. The gate closes automatically after the delay whether the remote was used or the gate was opened by any other means. This removes the risk of leaving the gate open through forgetfulness.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Access Control That Adds Security Value' },
    { type: 'image', src: '/images/gates/gate-proximity-fob-reader-closeup.png', alt: 'Close-up of proximity key fob access reader mounted on a gate post' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A video intercom with recording capability is the most security-relevant access control addition beyond basic remote controls. A camera at the gate records activity regardless of whether anyone answers, creating both a deterrent and an evidence source. Push notifications to a smartphone give real-time awareness of gate activity from anywhere. Activity logs from keypad and proximity reader systems show a timestamped record of every opening event.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Gate Construction and Physical Resistance' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A [heavy wrought iron or steel gate](/services/metal-driveway-gates/) on reinforced posts offers more physical resistance than a lightweight aluminium gate on standard hinges. However, the primary security benefit of a residential gate in the Kent context is deterrence rather than physical resistance. A gate of 1.8 metres or above is significantly harder to scale and is the standard recommendation for security-priority installations. Most Kent planning authorities accept 1.8 metre gates under permitted development.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Insurance Benefits' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Some vehicle insurers offer [reduced premiums for vehicles kept behind a closed electric gate](/blog/do-electric-gates-add-value-to-your-house/). The extent of any reduction varies by insurer and policy. Documenting the installation with photographs and commissioning certificates is worth doing if you intend to claim a security-related premium reduction. Check with your insurer before and after installation.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};

const article11: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'how-wide-should-driveway-gates-be-measuring-guide',
  title: 'How Wide Should Driveway Gates Be and How to Measure Your Entrance Correctly',
  metaTitle: 'How Wide Should Driveway Gates Be | Measuring Guide for Kent Homeowners',
  metaDescription: 'Getting the width wrong is the most common driveway gate mistake. This guide covers standard widths, how to measure your opening accurately, and what Kent installers need from you before quoting.',
  category: 'Buying Guide',
  publishDate: '2026-03-05',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-opening-driver-pov-tree-lined-driveway.png',
  excerpt: 'The width of your driveway gates affects everything from the motor specification to the post foundations. Getting it wrong is expensive to fix after installation. Here is how to get it right first time.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'Getting the gate width wrong is one of the most common and most expensive mistakes in residential gate installation. Gates that are too narrow force careful positioning every time you drive through, damage wing mirrors on a regular basis, and make life difficult for larger vehicles like delivery vans and skips. Gates that are unnecessarily wide cost more to fabricate, need heavier motors to drive them, put greater load on the post foundations, and can look out of proportion with the entrance. The right width is determined by your actual traffic requirements, the physical dimensions of your driveway, and the type of gate you are installing.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'This guide covers the standard widths that work for different situations, explains how to measure your driveway opening accurately before an installer visits, and highlights the Kent-specific factors that affect gate width decisions on properties across the county.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What Width Do You Actually Need' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The minimum clear opening for a single car to pass through comfortably is 2.7 metres. That is the gap between the gate posts when the gate is fully open, not the width of the gate leaves themselves. At 2.7 metres, a standard family car can pass without the driver needing to line up carefully, but there is no room for error with a wider vehicle. Most homeowners find 3 metres a more practical minimum for single-vehicle access because it provides a small margin for SUVs, estate cars, and the occasional van.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'For two vehicles to pass comfortably at the gate, or for a large vehicle like a removal lorry or skip truck to enter without difficulty, you need a clear opening of at least 4.5 metres. Most residential driveways in Kent that cater for two-car households are built between 3.5 and 4.5 metres wide, which is the range that the majority of domestic gate installations fall within.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Estate-scale entrances, which are common on larger properties in the Sevenoaks district, Tunbridge Wells, and the rural Weald, often run to 5, 6, or even 7 metres. At these widths the structural and motor specification changes significantly, and the gate type choice (swing versus sliding) is influenced as much by engineering as by appearance.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Single Gates vs Double Gates and How Width Affects the Choice' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A [single swing gate](/services/electric-swing-gates/) is practical up to about 2.5 metres wide. Beyond that, the weight of a single leaf becomes a problem for the hinge posts, and the arc clearance needed for a single large leaf sweeping through 90 degrees takes up a large amount of driveway space. The vast majority of swing gate installations across Kent use a double (bi-parting) configuration, with two leaves meeting at the centre and opening outward in opposite directions. A 3.6 metre opening, for example, is served by two 1.8 metre leaves, each light enough for standard hinges and motors to handle comfortably.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'For [sliding gates](/blog/electric-sliding-vs-swing-gates-kent/), width is less of a structural constraint because the gate travels horizontally rather than swinging on a hinge point. A sliding gate can comfortably span 5 or 6 metres as a single leaf. The trade-off is run-back space: the gate needs room to retract to one side, and that room must be at least as wide as the gate itself. A 5 metre sliding gate needs 5.5 metres of clear boundary to one side. If that space is not available, a biparting sliding configuration (two leaves, each retracting in opposite directions) halves the run-back requirement.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'How to Measure Your Driveway Opening Before the Site Survey' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Your installer will take precise measurements during the site survey, but having your own measurements ready saves time, helps you discuss options before the visit, and ensures you can compare quotes meaningfully. Here is how to measure correctly.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Start with the clear opening between the inside faces of whatever currently defines the entrance. If you have existing pillars, measure between the inside faces of the pillars at the narrowest point. Brick pillars often taper slightly or have a plinth that projects, so measure at the point where the gate will actually swing or slide, typically 300mm to 500mm above ground level. If you have no pillars and the entrance is simply a gap in a wall, hedge, or fence, measure the full width of the gap at the same height.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Write down three measurements. The width at ground level, the width at 500mm above ground, and the width at 1 metre above ground. On many Kent driveways, especially those with older brick pillars, these three figures are not the same. If the pillars lean or the brickwork is uneven, the narrowest measurement is the one that matters for gate sizing. Your installer will assess whether the pillars need straightening or rebuilding, but knowing the current dimensions upfront is useful.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Next, measure the depth available for the gate to open into. For swing gates, stand at the gate line and measure the distance into the driveway before you hit the first obstruction: a parked car, a wall return, a raised border, or a change in level. Each gate leaf needs to swing through a full 90-degree arc without hitting anything. For a double gate with 1.8 metre leaves, you need at least 1.8 metres of completely clear space behind the gate line on the driveway side.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'For sliding gates, measure the available run-back along the boundary on one or both sides of the opening. Walk along the boundary wall or fence line and note where it ends, where an obstruction occurs, or where the ground level changes. A clear, level run of boundary at least as wide as the gate is the minimum. If you have run-back available on both sides, note both measurements, as a biparting layout may be an option.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Width and Motor Specification' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Gate width directly affects motor choice and cost. A wider gate is a heavier gate, and a heavier gate needs a more powerful motor to open and close it reliably over a service life of ten years or more. For swing gates, the motor torque rating is calculated from the gate leaf weight and the leaf width combined, because a wider leaf creates more leverage at the hinge point. A motor that is adequate for a 1.5 metre leaf at 80 kilograms may not cope with a 2 metre leaf at 120 kilograms, even though the weight increase is not dramatic, because the leverage increase is.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Underground motors, which are the preferred specification across the premium West Kent market in Sevenoaks and Tunbridge Wells, have defined torque limits based on the model. Exceeding them shortens the motor life and increases the risk of a stall in cold weather when the lubricant is thicker. Your installer will match the motor to the gate dimensions and weight, which is one of the reasons a site survey before quoting is not optional. A motor specified from a phone conversation without seeing the gate is a motor that may be wrong.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Sliding gate motors work differently. The motor drives a pinion gear along a toothed rack fixed to the gate, and the power requirement scales with gate weight rather than width directly. However, wider sliding gates are heavier, so the relationship still holds. A 5 metre aluminium sliding gate and a 5 metre steel sliding gate of the same width require different motors because the steel gate weighs considerably more.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Width and Post Foundation Requirements' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The post foundation specification increases with gate width because wider gates exert more force on the posts during opening and closing, and more wind load when closed. A pair of 1.5 metre swing gate leaves on brick pillars with modest foundations may be perfectly stable. The same pillars supporting 2.5 metre leaves will be under significantly greater stress, particularly in the wind exposure that many Kent properties experience on the North Downs ridge and along the coast.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'New post foundations for gates wider than 4 metres typically require reinforced concrete pads at least 600mm deep and 400mm square, with steel reinforcement tied into the post. The ground conditions matter here. The chalk subsoil common across the North Downs drains well and provides stable bearing, but the Wealden clay that underlies much of the south of the county holds water and can move seasonally. An installer familiar with Kent ground conditions will specify the foundation depth and reinforcement for the specific soil type at your property.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Width Restrictions from Planning and Conservation Areas' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'In most of Kent, there is no planning restriction on the width of a residential driveway gate provided it remains under 2 metres in height and opens inward. However, properties within conservation areas, both National Landscapes, and the Green Belt along the M25 corridor may face additional requirements. Some conservation area supplementary planning documents specify that new boundary treatments should be in keeping with the prevailing character of the street, which can influence the scale and proportions of a gate entrance.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Listed building consent, which applies to any external alteration to a listed building, may include conditions on gate width where the entrance is part of the listed curtilage. Canterbury, Faversham, Tenterden, and dozens of Kent villages contain listed properties where these rules apply. Your installer should confirm the planning position at the survey. If there is any doubt, a pre-application enquiry to the relevant district council is the right first step and typically costs nothing.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'The Most Common Width Mistakes and How to Avoid Them' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The first mistake is measuring the gap between the outside faces of the pillars rather than the inside faces. The outside measurement includes the pillar width on both sides and will be 400mm to 600mm wider than the actual clear opening. A gate ordered to this measurement will not fit.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The second mistake is not accounting for hinge and motor clearance. A swing gate leaf does not hang flush with the inside face of the pillar. The hinge mechanism, whether a standard gate hinge or an underground motor pivot, offsets the gate face from the pillar by 30mm to 80mm depending on the hardware. On a double gate, this offset exists on both sides, reducing the effective clear opening by 60mm to 160mm from the post-to-post measurement. Your installer will factor this into the gate sizing, but it is worth understanding if you are comparing quotes that express width differently.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The third mistake is sizing the gate for the car you drive today without considering the vehicles that will need to pass through over the gate lifespan. A gate installed in 2026 will still be in service in 2046. If there is any chance that a larger vehicle, a caravan, a horsebox, or even a wider SUV will need regular access in the future, sizing for that eventuality now is far cheaper than modifying the gate or the pillars later.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The fourth mistake, specific to sliding gates, is not verifying the run-back space before committing to a single-leaf design. The installer checks this at the survey, but if you already know that the boundary wall ends 3 metres from the opening and you want a 4 metre gate, you can save everyone time by flagging the constraint upfront.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What to Have Ready Before Your Installer Visits' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The most useful information you can provide ahead of the site survey is a set of measurements and a few photographs. Measure the opening width at three heights as described above. Measure the depth available for swing clearance or the run-back available for sliding. Photograph the entrance from the road, from the driveway side, and from each side showing the boundary. Note any services you are aware of running under the driveway or near the pillar positions, including drainage, water supply, electricity, or telecoms. If you have existing pillars, note the material, approximate height, and whether they are plumb or leaning.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'None of this replaces a professional site survey. But it speeds up the survey, allows the installer to come prepared with initial ideas, and ensures the written quote you receive is based on the right dimensions from the start.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Getting a Width Recommendation for Your Kent Property' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Every property is different, and the right gate width for yours depends on the combination of driveway geometry, traffic requirements, property style, and any planning constraints that apply. A site survey is the only way to confirm the correct specification. Submit your details and we will match you with up to three Kent gate installers who will each visit the property, measure the opening professionally, and provide a written quote based on the dimensions your entrance actually needs.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};


const article12: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'do-electric-gates-add-value-to-your-house',
  title: 'Do Electric Gates Add Value to Your House or Are They Just a Nice Extra',
  metaTitle: 'Do Electric Gates Add Value to Your House | Kent Property Guide',
  metaDescription: 'Electric driveway gates can increase property value in Kent by 5% or more in the right area. This guide covers where gates add value, where they do not, and what buyers actually look for.',
  category: 'Buying Guide',
  publishDate: '2026-03-06',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-aerial-estate-closed-autumn-trees.png',
  excerpt: 'Estate agents in Kent regularly cite electric gates as a value-adding feature. But the reality is more nuanced than a simple yes or no. Where you live and what you install both matter.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'The question of whether electric gates add property value comes up in almost every conversation between a homeowner considering gates and the people advising them. Estate agents tend to say yes. Builders tend to shrug. The honest answer sits somewhere between the two and depends heavily on where in Kent your property is, what type of property it is, and what specification of gate you install.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'This guide breaks down the real-world impact of electric gates on Kent property values, based on what estate agents, valuers, and buyers in different parts of the county actually report. It also covers the situations where gates add less value than you might expect, and the specification choices that make the difference between a value-adding feature and an expensive addition that buyers discount.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Where Electric Gates Genuinely Add Property Value in Kent' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The areas where electric gates have the clearest positive impact on property value are the areas where buyers expect them. In the premium West Kent corridor around Sevenoaks, Westerham, Tunbridge Wells, and the surrounding villages, a detached property above a certain price threshold is expected to have gated access. A property at this level without gates can actually be perceived as incomplete by buyers, in the same way that a high-end kitchen without an island or a principal bedroom without an en-suite would be. The gates are not adding value so much as completing a specification that the market expects.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Estate agents working in the Sevenoaks and Tunbridge Wells market consistently report that electric gates on a property valued above roughly £800,000 are viewed by buyers as a standard feature rather than a premium addition. Below that threshold, gates are seen as a desirable extra. Above it, their absence is noticed. The value added in this market is estimated by local agents at between 3% and 5% of property value, though this figure is difficult to isolate from the other features that typically accompany gated properties.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Rural properties across the High Weald and Kent Downs National Landscapes also benefit from gated entrances in valuation terms. Buyers looking at farmhouses, oast houses, and barn conversions in these areas expect a level of privacy and boundary definition that a gated entrance provides. The aesthetic contribution matters here as much as the functional one. A well-specified hardwood or wrought iron gate that suits the character of the property and the landscape is perceived as part of the setting, not as an add-on.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Where Gates Add Less Value Than You Might Think' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'On properties in North Kent and the more urban parts of the county, electric gates are a less reliable value-add. A modern semi-detached house on an estate in Dartford or Gravesend with a short driveway and electric gates may actually raise questions in a buyer mind about why the gates were installed, with the assumption often being a security concern that itself becomes a negative signal about the area. This is not fair, but it is how some buyers think, and it affects perceived value.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Properties where the driveway is very short, where the gate installation looks like an afterthought rather than an integrated design, or where the gate material and style clash with the property character will not see a positive valuation impact. A pair of cheap steel gates on flimsy posts bolted to a Victorian brick wall actively detracts from the property presentation. Specification quality matters at least as much as the presence of gates.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'The Security Value and How Buyers Perceive It' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Security is the most commonly cited reason for installing electric gates across Kent, particularly in the Sevenoaks and Tunbridge Wells corridor where [vehicle theft from driveways](/blog/driveway-gates-home-security-kent/) is a documented concern. Buyers in these areas view electric gates with video intercom and auto-close as a meaningful security feature, and estate agents report that properties with this specification attract stronger initial interest and fewer price negotiations than equivalent properties without.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The security perception is strongest when the gate installation is visibly robust. Heavy wrought iron or steel gates on substantial pillars with a visible intercom panel signal that the property is protected. A lightweight aluminium gate on thin posts with a basic remote does not carry the same weight in a buyer assessment, even though the physical security difference between the two may be modest.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Video intercom systems with smartphone connectivity are increasingly viewed as a standard feature by buyers in the premium market. The ability to see and speak to visitors at the gate from anywhere, and to have a recorded log of gate activity, resonates with security-conscious buyers and is worth specifying on any installation where the property value justifies it.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What Specification Adds the Most Value' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Not all gate installations are equal in the eyes of a buyer or a valuer. The installations that add the most perceived value share several characteristics. The gate material and design are sympathetic to the property. The pillars are substantial and well-built, not lightweight additions bolted to existing walls. The [automation is concealed](/services/automated-gate-systems/) (underground motors rather than visible ram-arms on premium properties). The access control includes at least a video intercom. And the overall entrance design looks as though it was planned as part of the property rather than added later.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Wrought iron gates on brick pillars with underground motors consistently score highest in buyer perception surveys conducted by estate agents in the Surrey and Kent premium markets. Bespoke hardwood gates in conservation area and National Landscape settings score equally well because they demonstrate that the homeowner understood the planning context and invested in appropriate materials.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'At the other end, catalogue gates in standard sizes, visible ram-arm motors, basic remote-only access (no intercom), and pillars that clearly do not match the boundary or house construction all reduce the perceived value of the installation. In some cases, a poor-quality gate installation can reduce rather than increase the property appeal, because buyers assume it was done cheaply and may need replacing.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'The Insurance Angle' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Some home and vehicle insurance policies offer reduced premiums for properties with electric gates. The reduction is typically modest, but over the 20-year life of a gate installation it contributes to the total return on investment. The key requirement is that the gates must be automated (not manual), must auto-close after a set delay, and in some cases must include a video recording system at the entrance. Check with your insurer before installation to confirm what specification qualifies for a reduction and document the installation with photographs and commissioning certificates.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What a Valuer Actually Looks At' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'RICS surveyors and mortgage valuers assess electric gates as part of the overall property presentation and specification, not as a separately valued item. A gate installation that is consistent with the property standard and the area expectation is reflected positively in the valuation. One that is inconsistent, either too modest for the property or too elaborate for the area, may be noted but not valued.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Valuers also consider the condition of the installation. Gates that show signs of poor maintenance, rust on ungalvanised steel, peeling timber, or a motor that does not operate smoothly during the inspection will not add value regardless of what they cost to install. Maintaining the gate to a sellable standard throughout its life is part of protecting the investment.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'The Return on Investment Calculation' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A quality electric gate installation in Kent costs between £4,000 and £12,000 depending on the specification. On a property valued at £600,000 in a West Kent village, a 3% value uplift is £18,000. Even at the higher end of installation cost, the return is positive. On a property valued at £350,000 in a North Kent suburb, a 1% uplift is £3,500, which may not cover the installation cost if value appreciation is the sole motivation.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The calculation changes when you factor in [the daily convenience, the security benefit](/blog/do-electric-gates-add-value-to-your-house/), the insurance saving, and the aesthetic improvement to the property. Most homeowners who install electric gates in Kent do so primarily for these reasons, with the property value impact as a secondary benefit. If you are installing gates purely as a financial investment with no intention of living with them, the return depends entirely on the property location and the specification quality.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Privacy, Noise, and Boundary Control' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Solid or close-boarded gates provide complete visual screening of the driveway from the road. On Kent properties fronting busy A-roads or in villages with consistent through-traffic, this privacy has real quality-of-life value. A solid hardwood gate also reduces road noise reaching the front of the house, which can be significant on properties close to the A21, A26, or the M20 corridor.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Gates also establish a clear boundary that discourages casual use of your driveway for turning, parking, or access. This is a common frustration on properties in busy Kent villages and near schools, shops, or stations, where an ungated driveway becomes an informal turning circle. A closed gate eliminates this entirely.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'When Gates May Not Be Worth It' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If you have no manual gates currently and the driveway is very short (under 5 metres from the road to the house), the gate will be close to the building and may not provide meaningful security or privacy benefit. The installation cost remains the same but the practical return is lower.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If the primary motivation is property value and the property is below the threshold where buyers expect gates, the investment is unlikely to produce a positive return at resale. The money may be better spent on other improvements that have more reliable value impact at that price point.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If you are in a conservation area or National Landscape and the planning constraints would force a gate design that you do not actually like, the installation becomes a compromise that costs significant money but does not deliver the entrance you wanted. In this situation, it is worth exploring the planning position thoroughly before committing, and accepting that gates may not be practical if the constraints are too restrictive.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Getting the Right Gate for Your Kent Property' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The gates that add the most value are the ones that look right on the property and are built to last. That means choosing materials and a design that suit the house, the street, and the area. It means specifying hot-dip galvanising on steel, quality timber species on hardwood, and a motor from a manufacturer with long-term parts support. And it means working with an installer who understands Kent properties, Kent planning rules, and the expectations of Kent buyers.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Submit your details and we will match you with up to three Kent gate installers who will survey your property, recommend the right specification for your entrance, and provide written quotes that you can compare at your own pace. The matching service and every site survey are completely free.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};

const article13: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'how-long-do-electric-gates-last-before-replacing',
  title: 'How Long Do Electric Gates Last Before They Need Replacing',
  metaTitle: 'How Long Do Electric Gates Last | Lifespan Guide for Kent Homeowners',
  metaDescription: 'A well-specified electric gate should last 20 to 30 years for the gate itself and 10 to 15 years for the motor. This guide covers what affects lifespan and how to get the most from your installation.',
  category: 'Maintenance',
  publishDate: '2026-03-07',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-wrought-iron-detail-spear-finials-closeup.png',
  excerpt: 'Electric gates are a significant investment. Knowing how long the gate, the motor, and the access control will last helps you plan maintenance and budget for eventual replacement.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'The lifespan of an electric gate is not a single number because the system has several components that age at different rates. The gate structure itself, whether timber, steel, aluminium, or wrought iron, has the longest potential life. The motor and gearbox sit in the middle. The electronic control board, safety sensors, and access control devices have the shortest expected service life. Understanding these different timelines helps you plan servicing, anticipate replacement costs, and make specification decisions at the point of installation that pay off over the full ownership period.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'How Long the Gate Structure Lasts' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A hot-dip galvanised and powder-coated steel gate will last 25 to 40 years before the structure itself needs attention beyond cosmetic maintenance. The galvanising protects the steel from corrosion even if the powder coat is chipped or scratched, and the combination provides a service life that outlasts most other external fixtures on a residential property. Aluminium gates have an even longer structural life because aluminium does not corrode. There is no practical upper limit on how long an aluminium gate structure will last if it is not physically damaged.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Wrought iron, properly maintained, will last indefinitely. There are wrought iron gates on English properties that are over a hundred years old and still structurally sound. The key is the maintenance cycle. Wrought iron needs repainting or re-coating every 8 to 12 years to prevent rust from establishing itself in the surface. Once corrosion penetrates deeply into the iron, it becomes a structural problem rather than a cosmetic one.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Hardwood gates have a lifespan determined almost entirely by the timber species and the maintenance regime. Iroko and European oak, properly maintained with a penetrating oil every one to two years, will last 25 to 30 years. Accoya, with its modified cell structure, carries a manufacturer guarantee of 50 years above ground. Softwood gates, unless treated with a preservative system and maintained religiously, may show significant deterioration within 10 to 15 years in Kent conditions. For coastal East Kent properties, salt air exposure accelerates timber breakdown on species that are not naturally durable, making Accoya or a hardwood species the only sensible specification.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'How Long the Motor and Gearbox Last' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A quality gate motor from an established manufacturer (FAAC, BFT, CAME, Nice, or Beninca) has a typical working life of 10 to 15 years in residential use. That assumes it is correctly specified for the gate weight, properly installed, and serviced annually. The gearbox is usually the component that wears first, followed by the drive mechanism (the arm on a swing motor or the pinion on a sliding motor). Both are replaceable without changing the entire motor unit in most cases.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Motors that are undersized for the gate they are driving will fail sooner. A motor working close to its maximum torque rating on every cycle generates more heat, wears the gears faster, and stresses the control board. This is one of the most common causes of premature motor failure across Kent installations and is entirely avoidable with correct specification at the installation stage.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Underground motors, which are the standard in the premium West Kent market, are exposed to moisture in the chamber. The housing is sealed, but over a 10 to 15 year period, seals degrade and moisture can reach the internal components. Annual servicing that includes a check of the chamber drainage and seal condition extends motor life significantly. An underground motor that is never serviced in a poorly drained chamber may fail in 6 to 8 years.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'How Long the Electronics and Access Control Last' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Control boards typically last 8 to 12 years before the capacitors or relays degrade to the point where the board becomes unreliable. Replacement boards for the main motor brands are readily available and cost £150 to £350 fitted. This is a routine replacement rather than a system failure.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Photocell sensors last 10 to 15 years in most environments but can fail sooner in coastal positions where salt air corrodes the contacts. Video intercom systems have a shorter technology cycle. The hardware may last 10 years, but the software, app support, and connectivity standards may become obsolete in 5 to 7 years, requiring an upgrade to maintain smartphone integration. Keypad and proximity reader hardware is robust and typically lasts as long as the motor.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What Shortens the Life of Electric Gates' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The single biggest factor that shortens gate lifespan is [lack of annual servicing](/services/gate-repair-and-maintenance/). A gate system that is serviced every year will last 50% to 100% longer than an identical system that is never serviced. The second factor is incorrect specification at installation. A motor that is undersized, a gate that is not galvanised, hinges that are too light for the gate weight, and foundations that are too shallow for the soil conditions all create problems that compound over time.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Environmental exposure matters in Kent specifically. Properties on the North Downs ridge face more wind load than sheltered valley positions. Coastal properties between Whitstable and Folkestone face salt corrosion that inland properties do not. Properties surrounded by mature deciduous trees will have debris in sliding gate tracks and leaf litter around motor housings that accelerates wear if not cleared regularly.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'The Replacement Cycle for a Typical Kent Installation' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A realistic replacement timeline for a well-specified and annually serviced electric gate system in Kent looks something like this. The gate structure lasts 25 years or more with no intervention beyond cosmetic touch-ups. The motor and gearbox need a major service or replacement at 10 to 15 years. The control board is replaced once during the gate life. Photocells and safety edges are replaced once. The video intercom may be upgraded once or twice as technology moves on. Remote handsets are replaced as needed, typically every 5 to 8 years as batteries and electronics age.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The total cost of ownership over a 25-year period, including the original installation, annual servicing, and component replacements, is roughly 1.5 to 2 times the original installation cost. On a £6,000 installation, expect to spend £3,000 to £6,000 on maintenance and replacement parts over the following 25 years. The annual cost of ownership works out at £360 to £480 per year, which most Kent homeowners consider reasonable for the convenience and security that automated gates provide.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'How to Maximise the Life of Your Gates' },
    { type: 'p', text: '[Book annual servicing](/blog/electric-gate-maintenance-kent/) and do not skip it. Use an engineer who carries force measurement equipment and tests the safety systems properly, not just someone who lubricates the hinges and leaves. Keep sliding gate tracks clear of debris. Check hardwood gates for finish deterioration every spring and re-oil before the timber shows signs of drying. Report any unusual noise, slow operation, or erratic behaviour immediately rather than continuing to use the system until it fails completely. Address hinge wear at the point when it first becomes visible rather than waiting for the gate to drop.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'At the specification stage, invest in the right motor for the gate weight, hot-dip galvanising on all steel components, quality timber species rather than treated softwood, and a motor brand with established long-term parts availability. The cost difference between a correctly specified installation and a budget one is typically 15% to 25% of the total. The lifespan difference is often double.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Getting the Right Specification for Long-Term Value' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The installers in our Kent network specify for longevity as standard. Hot-dip galvanising on every steel gate. Motor brands with proven parts support for at least a decade. Timber species appropriate for the site exposure. Foundation depth matched to Kent soil conditions. Submit your enquiry and we will match you with up to three specialists who will survey your property and quote for an installation built to last.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};

const article14: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'best-electric-gate-motor-brands-uk-compared',
  title: 'The Best Electric Gate Motor Brands in the UK and What Sets Them Apart',
  metaTitle: 'Best Electric Gate Motor Brands UK | FAAC vs BFT vs CAME vs Nice 2026',
  metaDescription: 'FAAC, BFT, CAME, Nice, and Beninca dominate the UK residential gate motor market. This guide compares them on reliability, parts availability, features, and which suits different gate types.',
  category: 'Automation',
  publishDate: '2026-03-08',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-underground-motor-installation-engineer.png',
  excerpt: 'The motor is the hardest-working component in your gate system. Choosing the right brand affects reliability, parts availability, and how long the system lasts. Here is how the main brands compare.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'The gate motor market in the UK is dominated by five Italian manufacturers. FAAC, BFT, CAME, Nice, and Beninca between them account for the vast majority of [residential gate automation](/services/automated-gate-systems/) installed across Kent and the wider South East. All five produce motors that are technically competent and well-supported. The differences between them are real but more nuanced than a simple ranking from best to worst. The right brand for your project depends on your gate type, the weight of the gate, whether you want underground or surface-mounted motors, and what access control features matter to you.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'This guide compares the five main brands on the factors that matter most to Kent homeowners. Reliability, parts availability in the UK, the range of residential models, smart home integration, and the experience that local installers have with each brand.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'FAAC' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'FAAC is the oldest of the five, founded in Bologna in 1965, and has the largest installed base in the UK. Their underground swing motors (the 770 and 780 series) are the most widely specified underground units on premium residential properties across Kent, particularly in the Sevenoaks and Tunbridge Wells market where concealed automation is the standard expectation. FAAC motors have a reputation for robust gearbox engineering and long service intervals.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Parts availability for FAAC in the UK is excellent. Every gate engineer covering Kent stocks FAAC components. The control boards, motor units, and drive arms are all individually replaceable, which means a component failure at year 8 or 10 does not require a full motor replacement. FAAC also offers a Wi-Fi module (the FAAC Connect) that allows smartphone operation and integrates with Google Home and Amazon Alexa.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The main criticism of FAAC from installers is that the control board interface is less intuitive than some competitors, making initial programming slightly more involved. This is an installer concern rather than a homeowner concern, but it means that an installer unfamiliar with FAAC may take longer to commission the system.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'BFT' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'BFT, also Italian, has built a strong position in the UK residential market over the past fifteen years. Their underground motors (the ELI and SUB ranges) compete directly with FAAC on performance and are often slightly more competitive on price. BFT has invested heavily in connectivity, and their U-Link platform offers one of the more polished smartphone apps in the gate motor market, with real-time gate status, event logging, and remote access management.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'BFT parts are widely stocked by Kent gate engineers, though not quite as universally as FAAC. The brand is particularly well-regarded for its sliding gate motors, where the DEIMOS and ARES ranges are considered among the most reliable rack-and-pinion drives available for residential use. BFT also offers a solar power kit that integrates with their motor range, which is relevant for rural Kent properties without convenient mains access at the gate position.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'CAME' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'CAME is one of the largest automation manufacturers globally and has a significant presence in the UK market. Their FROG underground motor is a long-established product with a proven track record on residential properties. CAME has the broadest product range of the five brands, covering everything from small residential swing gates to heavy commercial sliding systems, which means they have a motor for virtually any gate specification.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'CAME Connect is their smart home platform, offering app-based control and integration with third-party systems. Parts availability in the UK is good, supported by CAME UK, their dedicated subsidiary. The brand is slightly less prevalent among Kent residential specialists than FAAC or BFT but is well-established and fully supported. CAME is often the preferred specification for larger or heavier gates where the motor needs to be at the upper end of the torque range.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Nice' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Nice (pronounced Nee-chay, not the English word) is the most design-conscious of the five brands. Their motor housings are more compact and visually refined than most competitors, which matters on installations where the motor is surface-mounted and visible from the property side. The Nice Era range of surface-mounted swing motors is particularly popular on [Kent retrofit projects](/blog/electric-gate-automation-retrofit-kent/) where aesthetics matter but underground installation is not practical.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Nice was early to smart home integration and their app platform is mature and well-supported. Their Yubii home automation system allows gate control alongside other connected devices. Parts availability in the UK is good through Nice UK, though slightly less universal at the van-stock level among Kent engineers than FAAC. Nice motors tend to be quietly engineered, running with less noise than some competitors, which is a genuine advantage on gates close to bedroom windows.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Beninca' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Beninca is the smallest of the five in the UK market but has a loyal following among installers who rate the engineering quality highly. Their underground motors are robustly built and their sliding gate drives are well-regarded for heavy-duty residential applications. Beninca is often specified on projects where the gate is exceptionally heavy or where the duty cycle is higher than typical residential use.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The trade-off with Beninca is parts availability. While the brand is fully supported in the UK, van stock among Kent engineers is less comprehensive than for FAAC or BFT. A repair on a Beninca system may occasionally require a parts order and a return visit where a FAAC or BFT repair could be completed the same day from van stock. This is a practical consideration worth discussing with your installer.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Which Brand Should You Choose' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'For most Kent residential projects, the honest answer is that any of the five main brands will serve you well if the motor is correctly specified for the gate weight and type. The more important decisions are whether the motor is the right size and type for your gate, whether the installer is experienced with the brand they are proposing, and whether the brand has long-term parts support in the UK.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If you are installing underground swing motors on a premium West Kent property, FAAC and BFT are the two brands with the deepest local installer experience and the most comprehensive van stock. If you need a surface-mounted motor where appearance matters, Nice offers the most refined housing design. If your gate is exceptionally heavy or large, CAME and Beninca both offer models at the top of the residential torque range. If smart home integration is a priority, BFT and Nice currently offer the most polished app platforms.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What to Ask Your Installer About the Motor' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'When your installer recommends a specific motor, ask three questions. First, is the motor torque rating adequate for your gate weight with a safety margin, or is it running close to its maximum. Second, does the installer stock replacement parts for this brand and model on the van, or will a repair require ordering parts. Third, what is the manufacturer parts support commitment for this model, specifically how many years after production ends will components still be available. The answers to these questions will tell you more about the long-term reliability of your installation than any brand comparison.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A good installer will recommend the right motor for your gate after a physical site assessment. Submit your enquiry and we will match you with up to three specialists who will survey your property and specify the motor to suit your gate, your access control requirements, and your budget.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};

const article16: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'driveway-gate-ideas-for-older-houses-and-period-properties',
  title: 'Driveway Gate Ideas for Older Houses and Period Properties in Kent',
  metaTitle: 'Driveway Gate Ideas for Period Properties Kent | Design Inspiration 2026',
  metaDescription: 'Choosing gates for a Victorian, Edwardian, Georgian, or medieval Kent property? This guide covers design principles, materials, planning rules, and how to get the aesthetic right.',
  category: 'Design',
  publishDate: '2026-03-12',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-steel-sage-green-swing-period-brick-spring.png',
  excerpt: 'Period properties need gates that respect the architecture without looking like a museum piece. The right design references the building without copying it, and the material should belong in the setting.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'Kent has one of the densest concentrations of period residential properties in England. The medieval streets of Canterbury and Sandwich, the Georgian and Victorian terraces of Tunbridge Wells, the Edwardian suburbs of Sevenoaks and Tonbridge, and the Wealden farmsteads and oast houses scattered across the countryside from Cranbrook to Tenterden all create contexts where a gate needs to do more than close off a driveway. It needs to sit within the visual language of the building and the street without drawing attention to itself as something new.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Getting this right requires understanding what the property is saying architecturally and designing a gate that responds to it. Getting it wrong results in a gate that jars with the house, annoys the neighbours, and in conservation areas may not pass planning scrutiny at all. This guide covers the design principles, material choices, and practical considerations for gates on older Kent houses.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'The First Principle is to Read the Building' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Before thinking about gate styles, look at the house and the boundary as they are now. What materials are used on the front elevation? Red brick, ragstone, render, tile-hanging, timber frame, flint? What is the existing boundary treatment? A brick wall, a hedge, iron railings, post and rail, close board fencing? What is the scale of the entrance relative to the house? A modest cottage with a narrow driveway and a low wall calls for a very different gate from a substantial Victorian villa set behind tall brick piers on a wide entrance.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The gate should feel as though it could have been there since the house was built, even though it obviously was not. This does not mean it has to be a period reproduction. It means the proportions, the material, and the level of detail should be consistent with the building and the boundary character. A pair of iroko gates with a simple frame and tongue-and-groove infill can look entirely right on a Victorian house without referencing any specific Victorian gate design.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Wrought Iron and Fabricated Steel for Town Houses' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The Victorian and Edwardian houses that line the streets of Sevenoaks, Tunbridge Wells, Tonbridge, and the older parts of Canterbury often have existing iron railings at the front boundary. Where these survive, a wrought iron or fabricated steel gate that matches the profile, the rail spacing, and the finial style of the railings is the obvious and usually the correct approach. A good fabricator can take a section of existing railing as a reference and produce a gate that appears to have been made by the same blacksmith.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Where the original railings have been lost, which happened to many Kent properties during wartime scrap drives, the gate design can reference the period without reproducing a specific historic pattern. Vertical round bars with spear or ball finials, a curved top rail, and a simple scroll detail at the hinge point are all vocabulary elements of Victorian ironwork that work on a new gate without looking like a pastiche. Sage green, black, and dark blue are the period powder coat colours most commonly specified on Kent conservation area properties.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Hardwood for Rural and Wealden Properties' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The oast houses, timber-frame farmhouses, and barn conversions of the Kent Weald call for hardwood as naturally as the town houses call for iron. [European oak](/services/wooden-driveway-gates/) is the premium timber choice for this context because its grain character and weathering behaviour match the aged timber already present on most Wealden buildings. Left untreated, oak develops a consistent silver patina over two to three years that harmonises with the building. Oiled, it holds a warm brown tone that works against both ragstone and brick.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The gate design for a Wealden property should be simple rather than ornate. Close-boarded for privacy, or open-framed with a top rail and vertical boards if the property has an open boundary character. The detailing should be understated. A chamfered edge on the boards, a simple curved top rail, and traditional strap hinges visible on the face of the gate are all sufficient to establish character without overworking the design. Elaborate carving, turned spindles, and decorative cutouts generally look wrong on agricultural conversions and rural farmhouses.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Georgian Properties and the Question of Proportion' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Georgian houses are defined by symmetry and proportion. The gate entrance should respect this. Paired gates of equal width, centred on the driveway, with pillar heights that relate to the window heights and roofline of the house. Georgian ironwork is typically simpler and more restrained than Victorian. Straight vertical bars with minimal decoration, a flat or gently curved top rail, and ball or urn finials rather than ornate scrollwork. The elegance comes from the proportion rather than the detail.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'On Georgian properties in Kent, particularly in the Canterbury and Faversham areas where Georgian and Regency architecture is well represented, the pillar specification is as important as the gate itself. Brick pillars with a stone or cast cap, proportioned to the gate height and the boundary wall height, frame the entrance correctly. Thin steel posts or lightweight timber posts undermine the formality that a Georgian entrance requires.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Medieval and Tudor Properties' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Canterbury and the surrounding villages contain a significant number of medieval and Tudor properties where the boundary treatment is part of the listed curtilage. Listed building consent is required for any gate installation on these properties, and the design must satisfy the conservation officer. Timber is almost always the appropriate material. The style should be robust and simple, reflecting the vernacular building tradition rather than any formal gate design. Thick oak boards, iron strap hinges, and a substantial top rail reference the construction methods of the building itself.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'On these properties, the gate is often relatively modest in scale compared to later periods. A 1.2 to 1.5 metre height is common, with the gate sitting within rather than above the boundary wall. Automation needs to be completely invisible. Underground motors or, where the wall structure prevents excavation, a concealed system within the gate leaf or post, are the only acceptable approaches.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Conservation Area Rules in Kent' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Canterbury, Faversham, Tenterden, Sandwich, Sevenoaks, Tunbridge Wells, and dozens of Kent villages have conservation area designations that affect what you can install at the property boundary. In a conservation area, [permitted development rights for gates](/blog/planning-permission-driveway-gates-kent/) remain in force, but the local planning authority can impose Article 4 Directions that remove these rights and require a planning application for boundary treatments.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Even where permitted development applies, it is worth designing the gate with the conservation area character in mind. A gate that provokes complaints from neighbours or an enquiry from the conservation officer creates unnecessary stress, even if it turns out to be technically permitted. Matching the material and style to the prevailing boundary character of the street is the simplest way to avoid this. Your installer should be able to advise on what the local character requires based on their experience working in the area.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Automation That Stays Hidden' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'On period properties, visible automation equipment detracts from the entrance. Underground motors are the standard solution and work on the majority of period property installations where the post foundations can accommodate the chamber. Where underground installation is not possible, ram-arm motors can be positioned on the inside face of the gate and post, invisible from the street. Some installers in our Kent network have experience with concealed motor systems built into the gate leaf itself, though these are specialist installations.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Access control hardware should also be discreet. A video intercom panel can be flush-mounted into a brick pillar or wall rather than surface-mounted on a visible bracket. Proximity readers can be concealed within the post cap. Keypads should be finished in a colour that matches the pillar material rather than left in standard white or silver plastic. Every detail that keeps the technology invisible improves the overall presentation of the entrance.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Getting Professional Design Input' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The best gate installations on period Kent properties involve a design conversation between the homeowner, the installer, and in some cases the fabricator or joinery workshop. Drawings produced before fabrication allow you to see and adjust the design before any material is cut or metal is bent. On larger projects, 3D renders showing the gate in position against the existing building are standard practice and worth requesting.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Submit your enquiry and we will match you with Kent gate specialists who have demonstrable experience with period properties, conservation areas, and listed buildings. Each one will visit the property, discuss the design brief, and produce drawings and a quote tailored to your specific entrance.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};

const article18: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'what-to-do-when-electric-gates-stop-working',
  title: 'What to Do When Your Electric Gates Stop Working',
  metaTitle: 'Electric Gates Not Working | Troubleshooting Guide for Kent Homeowners',
  metaDescription: 'Your electric gates have stopped. Before you call an engineer, there are several things you can check yourself and steps to take to keep the property secure while you wait for a repair.',
  category: 'Maintenance',
  publishDate: '2026-03-16',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-automation-commissioning-tablet-installer.png',
  excerpt: 'Electric gate failures are stressful, especially when the gate is stuck open and the property feels exposed. Here is what to do immediately, what to check yourself, and when to call a professional.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'An electric gate that stops working is one of those household failures that feels more urgent than it actually is, because the gate sits between your property and the public road and a gate stuck open creates an immediate feeling of vulnerability. The good news is that most gate faults are caused by a small number of well-understood problems, many of which can be identified or even resolved without an engineer visit. This guide walks you through what to do first, what to check yourself, and when you need professional help.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Step One is to Secure the Gate Manually' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Before diagnosing anything, get the gate into a secure position. Every automated gate system has a manual release mechanism that disengages the motor and allows the gate to be moved by hand. On swing gates, this is usually a key-operated release on the motor housing that frees the gate from the drive arm, allowing you to push the gate closed and latch it. On sliding gates, the release disengages the motor from the drive rack, allowing the gate to slide freely on the track.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The manual release procedure is specific to your motor brand and model. It should have been demonstrated at handover and documented in the paperwork your installer provided. If you cannot locate the instructions, search for the motor brand and model number followed by "manual release" and you will find the manufacturer documentation. [FAAC, BFT, CAME, Nice, and Beninca](/blog/best-electric-gate-motor-brands-uk-compared/) all publish release procedures for every motor in their residential range.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Once the gate is closed and latched manually, the security concern is resolved and you can take your time diagnosing the fault without pressure.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Check the Power Supply' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The most common reason for a gate that does not respond to any input is a power supply failure. Check the circuit breaker or fuse that supplies the gate motor at your property distribution board. If it has tripped, reset it. If it trips again immediately, there is a fault in the wiring or the motor that requires an engineer. Do not keep resetting a breaker that repeatedly trips.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If the breaker is fine, check whether there is a local isolator switch near the gate motor. Some installations have a weatherproof switch on the wall or pillar near the motor position that may have been accidentally turned off by a visitor, a gardener, or a child. It sounds obvious, but isolation switches account for a surprising number of "gate not working" calls to engineers across Kent.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Power cuts are another common cause. If the mains supply to the property has been interrupted and the battery backup has discharged, the gate will not operate until power is restored. Check whether other electrical items in the house are working. If the power is off, wait for it to return. The gate should resume normal operation automatically once the mains supply is restored and the battery has recharged, which typically takes 15 to 30 minutes.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Check the Remote Control' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If the gate does not respond to the remote but the control board lights are on (visible through the housing window on most motors), the fault may be with the remote rather than the gate. Replace the remote battery first. If you have a second remote, test with that. If neither works, try operating the gate from the button on the control board itself, if accessible. A gate that responds to the board button but not the remote has a remote or receiver fault, not a motor fault.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Remotes can lose their programming, particularly after a power interruption or a battery replacement. The re-programming procedure is in your motor handbook and typically involves pressing a button sequence on the control board while pressing the remote button. If you are not comfortable doing this, an engineer can re-programme the remotes in a few minutes during a callout.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Check for Obstructions in the Gate Path' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A gate that starts to move but stops and reverses is almost always responding to a safety system. The most common cause is a photocell beam that has been broken by something in the gate path, or a photocell that has shifted out of alignment. Look along the gate opening for anything that might be blocking the beam: a wheelie bin, a garden hose, a parcel left by a delivery driver, a build-up of leaves or debris around the photocell housing. Move anything in the path and try the gate again.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'On sliding gates, debris in the track channel can physically obstruct the gate travel. Leaves, gravel, small stones, and ice in winter all cause the gate to meet resistance, which triggers the auto-reverse safety feature. Clear the track channel along its full length and try again. This is the single most common cause of sliding gate faults in autumn across Kent properties surrounded by mature trees.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'When You Need an Engineer' },
    { type: 'p', text: '[Call a professional](/services/gate-repair-and-maintenance/) if the gate does not respond to any input and the power supply is confirmed as live. This indicates a control board, transformer, or motor fault that requires diagnostic equipment to identify. Call an engineer if the gate makes unusual mechanical noises such as grinding, scraping, or clicking, which indicate a gearbox, hinge, or drive mechanism issue that will worsen if the gate continues operating. Call an engineer if the gate operates but does not close fully, does not stop at the correct position, or reverses without visible cause after you have checked and cleared the photocell path.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Do not continue operating a gate that is making mechanical noises or behaving erratically. Switch to manual mode using the release and book a callout. Running a faulty motor accelerates wear and can turn a £200 repair into a £600 motor replacement.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What to Tell the Engineer When You Call' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The more information you give the engineer, the faster and cheaper the repair is likely to be. Tell them the motor brand and model (printed on the motor housing label). Describe the fault in specific terms. "The gate starts to close then reverses after two seconds" is more useful than "the gate is not working." Mention anything that happened immediately before the fault, such as a power cut, a vehicle clipping the gate, or a period of heavy rain. Note whether the gate responds to the remote, the wall button, or neither. And confirm whether you have been able to close the gate manually.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Engineers in our Kent network carry van stock for FAAC, BFT, CAME, Nice, and Beninca. If you can confirm the motor brand before the callout, the engineer can check that they have the likely parts on the van, which increases the chance of a first-visit repair.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Preventing Faults Before They Happen' },
    { type: 'p', text: '[Annual servicing](/blog/electric-gate-maintenance-kent/) catches the majority of developing faults before they cause a system failure. A service visit includes lubrication of the motor and drive mechanism, safety sensor testing and recalibration, battery backup load testing, hinge inspection, and track cleaning on sliding gates. It costs £120 to £210 in Kent and typically adds years to the working life of the motor while keeping the manufacturer warranty valid.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If your gate has not been serviced in the last 12 months, book a service rather than waiting for a failure. The service cost is a fraction of an emergency callout and repair, and the engineer will identify any components that are approaching the end of their service life before they fail unexpectedly.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Need a gate engineer in Kent? Submit your details and we will connect you with repair specialists covering your area who carry parts for all the main motor brands.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};

const article19: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'can-you-install-driveway-gates-on-a-sloped-driveway',
  title: 'Can You Install Driveway Gates on a Sloped Driveway',
  metaTitle: 'Driveway Gates on a Slope | Solutions for Sloped Kent Driveways',
  metaDescription: 'Sloped driveways across the North Downs and the Kent Weald create challenges for gate installation. This guide covers what works, what does not, and how experienced installers handle the gradient.',
  category: 'Buying Guide',
  publishDate: '2026-03-18',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-aerial-wrought-iron-closed-topiary-gravel-circle.png',
  excerpt: 'A sloped driveway does not rule out electric gates. It does rule out certain configurations and it does require an installer who has solved this problem before. Here is what you need to know.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'Sloped driveways are common across Kent. The North Downs chalk ridge creates sharp elevation changes between the road and properties along its length from Westerham through Sevenoaks, Wrotham, and onward to the coast above Folkestone. The Wealden clay hills between Tonbridge and Tenterden produce rolling terrain where driveways frequently slope, curve, or both. And many Kent villages sit on hillsides where the road is at one level and the property is at another.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The question is not whether gates can be installed on a sloped driveway. They can. The question is [which type of gate](/blog/electric-sliding-vs-swing-gates-kent/), which motor configuration, and which hinge geometry work on your specific gradient. Getting this right requires an installer who has worked on sloped sites before, because the engineering is different from a flat driveway and the consequences of getting it wrong are expensive to fix.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Why Slope Creates Problems for Swing Gates' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A swing gate opens in an arc. The bottom edge of the gate leaf traces a circular path as it swings from closed to open. On a flat driveway, this path stays at a consistent height above the ground and the clearance between the gate bottom and the surface is uniform throughout the arc. On a driveway that slopes, the ground surface rises or falls relative to the arc path, and the clearance changes.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If the driveway slopes downward from the road toward the house (the most common configuration in Kent, where properties sit below the road level), the ground drops away as the gate opens inward. The gate leaf clears the ground easily at the start of its travel but may have excessive clearance at the fully open position, which looks odd and compromises security at the bottom of the gate.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If the driveway slopes upward from the road (less common, but found on properties above road level on the North Downs), the ground rises into the arc path as the gate opens. The gate leaf may scrape or jam partway through its travel, and a standard motor will stall or reverse when it meets the resistance. This is the more problematic configuration and the one where a flat-driveway specification will fail.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Solutions for Swing Gates on Slopes' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The first solution is articulated hinges. These are hinges with a built-in pivot that lifts the gate leaf as it opens, compensating for the rising ground surface. The lift angle is set during installation to match the driveway gradient. Articulated hinges handle gradients up to approximately 10 degrees (about 1 in 6) and are the standard solution for moderate slopes across Kent.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The second solution is to adjust the motor force and travel settings to account for the gradient. A gate opening uphill requires more motor torque than the same gate on a flat surface. Underground motors with higher torque ratings, or ram-arm motors with adjustable force settings, can be tuned to drive the gate through its arc on a slope. This works in combination with articulated hinges on steeper gradients.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The third solution, for steep slopes where swing gates are impractical regardless of hinge engineering, is to switch to a sliding gate. This is the most common outcome on Kent driveways with gradients above 10 degrees.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Why Sliding Gates Handle Slopes Better' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A [sliding gate moves horizontally along the boundary](/services/electric-sliding-gates/) rather than swinging through an arc. It does not need to clear a changing ground surface because it travels parallel to the boundary, not perpendicular to the driveway. The track or cantilever rail is installed level along the boundary, and the gate slides along it regardless of what the driveway surface is doing in the perpendicular direction.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Ground-track systems require the track itself to be level, which may mean building up the track foundation on the lower side of a sloped boundary. This is standard groundwork and adds modest cost. Cantilever systems, which suspend the gate from an elevated rail rather than running it on a ground track, eliminate the ground-level constraint entirely and are the preferred configuration for the steepest Kent sites.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The trade-off with sliding gates is the run-back space requirement. The gate needs a [clear boundary to one side at least as wide as the gate leaf](/blog/how-wide-should-driveway-gates-be-measuring-guide/) plus approximately 500mm. On sloped sites, this boundary run needs to be level as well, which may require retaining work on sites where the boundary follows the slope of the land. Your installer will assess whether the run-back can be achieved within the existing boundary or whether modification is needed.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What Gradient Can Each System Handle' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Swing gates with standard hinges and standard motors work on gradients up to about 3 degrees (approximately 1 in 20). This covers driveways that have a gentle slope that most people would not think of as sloped.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Swing gates with articulated hinges and uprated motors handle gradients from 3 to 10 degrees (approximately 1 in 20 to 1 in 6). This covers the majority of noticeably sloped driveways in Kent, including most properties on the North Downs ridge and the Wealden hillsides.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Above 10 degrees, sliding gates with a cantilever system are the reliable solution. Properties with very steep driveways, which are found on the scarp face of the North Downs and on some of the steeper Wealden lanes, fall into this category. There is no practical upper gradient limit for a cantilever sliding gate because the gate travel is independent of the driveway surface.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'How an Installer Assesses a Sloped Site' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The site survey on a sloped driveway involves measuring the gradient at the gate line, the gradient across the full swing arc (for swing gates), the run-back availability and level (for sliding gates), the soil type (which affects foundation specification), and the available power supply position relative to the motor location. A good installer photographs the driveway from multiple angles and takes measurements with a spirit level or digital inclinometer rather than estimating by eye.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The assessment should result in a clear recommendation for gate type, motor configuration, and any additional groundwork required, along with a written quote that itemises these elements separately. If an installer quotes for a standard swing gate installation without mentioning the gradient or visiting the site, they are not accounting for the slope and the installation is likely to have problems.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'The Cost Difference on a Sloped Site' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Articulated hinges add £200 to £500 to a swing gate installation. Uprated motors or underground units with higher torque ratings may add £300 to £800 depending on the brand and model. A cantilever sliding gate system typically costs £800 to £2,500 more than a standard ground-track installation due to the heavier framework and the more complex foundation. These are real cost additions, but they are substantially less than the cost of installing a standard system, discovering that it does not work on the slope, and then modifying or replacing it.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Finding an Installer Who Knows Sloped Sites' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Experience with sloped driveways is not universal among gate installers. An installer who works primarily on flat suburban driveways in North Kent may not have encountered the gradient challenges that are routine on the North Downs or in the Weald. When we match you with installers, we account for site conditions including gradient, and connect you with firms that have handled sloped installations in your part of Kent.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Submit your enquiry with your postcode and a note about the driveway slope. Photographs showing the gradient from the road are helpful. We will match you with up to three Kent gate specialists who have the experience to get the specification right for your site.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};

const article20: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'how-to-look-after-wooden-driveway-gates',
  title: 'How to Look After Wooden Driveway Gates So They Last 25 Years or More',
  metaTitle: 'How to Maintain Wooden Driveway Gates | Care Guide for Kent Homeowners',
  metaDescription: 'Wooden driveway gates will last decades with the right maintenance. This guide covers oiling schedules, what to inspect, how to handle damage, and the differences between iroko, oak, and Accoya.',
  category: 'Maintenance',
  publishDate: '2026-03-20',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-wooden-iroko-closeup-kent-weald-countryside.png',
  excerpt: 'A hardwood gate is a long-term investment that rewards a modest maintenance commitment. Neglect it and you will see deterioration within five years. Look after it and it will outlast the house.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: '[Hardwood driveway gates](/services/wooden-driveway-gates/) are among the most durable external fixtures you can install on a Kent property. Iroko gates that were hung in the 1990s are still in sound condition across the Weald. European oak gates on Canterbury conservation area properties have decades of service ahead of them. Accoya gates installed in coastal East Kent carry a 50-year manufacturer guarantee that most homeowners will never need to test. But all of this [longevity](/blog/how-long-do-electric-gates-last-before-replacing/) depends on one condition. The gates need to be looked after.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The maintenance is not complicated and it is not expensive. It involves an annual inspection, a treatment application every one to two years for oiled timbers, and prompt attention to any damage or deterioration before it has a chance to worsen. This guide covers exactly what to do, when to do it, and how the three main timber species used on Kent gates differ in their maintenance requirements.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'The Annual Spring Inspection' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The best time to inspect your wooden gates is in early spring, after the winter weather has done its worst and before the growing season starts. Walk up to the gate and look at the whole structure from a few metres away first. Is the gate hanging level or has one leaf dropped? Are the gaps between the gate and the posts consistent or has something shifted? Does the gate close cleanly to the latch point or does it need forcing?' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Then look more closely. Check the surface finish. Has the oil or stain thinned out, particularly on the top edge and the end grain of the boards, which are the first places to lose protection? Are there any areas where the timber has turned grey, which indicates the surface treatment has broken down and the timber is weathering? Are there any splits or surface checks in the boards, particularly on the south-facing side that gets the most sun exposure? Is there any green algae or moss growth on the bottom rail or the lower boards, which indicates persistent dampness?' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Check the ironmongery. Are the hinges tight or is there play? Can you lift the gate on the hinge side? If there is movement, the hinge pins or the hinge bolts may need tightening or replacing. Check the latch and lock mechanism. Does it operate smoothly? Has moisture affected any of the metal components? On hot-dip galvanised ironmongery, the zinc coating should still be intact. On stainless steel fittings, check for any discolouration at the bolt holes that might indicate the start of crevice corrosion, particularly on coastal Kent properties.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'How to Oil Iroko Gates' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Iroko is the most common hardwood for Kent residential gates and it responds well to a penetrating oil treatment. The purpose of oiling is not to waterproof the timber, which iroko does naturally through its own oil content, but to maintain the surface appearance, prevent surface checking from UV exposure, and keep the timber looking warm rather than allowing it to grey.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Use a penetrating hardwood oil, not a surface varnish or paint. Osmo UV Protection Oil, Sikkens Cetol HLS Plus, and Owatrol Textrol are all widely used on iroko gates by Kent timber gate specialists. Teak oil from a hardware store also works but may need more frequent reapplication.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Apply in dry weather when the timber surface is dry and the temperature is above 10 degrees. Lightly sand any areas where the previous coat has broken down or where grey weathering is visible, using 120-grit sandpaper. Brush off the dust. Apply the oil with a wide brush, working it into the end grain, the top edge, and any joints first, as these are the areas that absorb moisture fastest. Then coat the flat faces of the boards. One coat is usually sufficient if you are maintaining an existing finish. Two coats if you are restoring a gate that has been left too long.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The treatment interval for iroko in Kent conditions is every 18 to 24 months. South-facing gates that get more sun exposure may need attention at the shorter end of this range. North-facing gates and gates sheltered by trees or buildings can go longer between treatments.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'How to Maintain European Oak Gates' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Oak is a more complex timber to maintain because homeowners have two valid choices. You can oil it to maintain a warm brown appearance, following essentially the same process as iroko. Or you can leave it untreated and allow it to weather naturally to a silver-grey patina. Both approaches are legitimate, and the choice depends on the look you want and the setting of the property.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If you choose to oil, the process is the same as for iroko but with one difference. Oak contains tannins that react with water and can produce dark staining on adjacent masonry if rainwater runs off untreated oak onto pale stone or render. Oiling the oak reduces this tannin leaching significantly. If your gate is hung next to pale brickwork or rendered pillars, oiling is the better choice for the first two to three years until the tannin levels in the surface timber have reduced.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If you choose the natural weathering route, the gate still needs an annual inspection for structural integrity. Check joints, check for splits that go through the full board thickness (surface checks are normal and not a concern), and check that the ironmongery is secure. The silvering process takes 18 to 36 months to reach a consistent colour across the whole gate. During this period the gate may look patchy as different faces weather at different rates. This is normal and resolves itself.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'How to Maintain Accoya Gates' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Accoya is the lowest-maintenance timber option for Kent gates, and this is one of the main reasons it is specified. The acetylation process that modifies the timber at a cellular level means it absorbs very little moisture, does not swell and shrink with seasonal humidity changes, and holds paint and stain finishes far longer than conventional timber.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If your Accoya gate is painted, the paint finish will typically last 8 to 10 years before it needs refreshing, compared to 2 to 4 years for paint on conventional softwood. If it is oiled or stained, the treatment interval is 4 to 6 years rather than the 18 to 24 months that iroko requires. The annual inspection described above still applies, but the hands-on maintenance is significantly less frequent.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'For Accoya gates on coastal Kent properties between Whitstable and Folkestone, the maintenance advantage is most pronounced. Salt air that would degrade a conventional softwood gate within a few years has minimal effect on Accoya because the timber simply does not absorb the salt-laden moisture in the way that untreated wood does.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Dealing with Damage and Deterioration' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Surface checks (small cracks that follow the grain on the face of the board) are normal on all hardwood gates and do not affect structural integrity. They are caused by the surface of the timber drying faster than the interior, and they typically open in dry weather and close in damp weather. They do not need repair. Filling them with a sealant actually traps moisture and makes the problem worse.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A split that goes through the full thickness of a board is different and should be addressed. Small splits can be stabilised with exterior-grade wood glue and a clamp. Larger splits may require the affected board to be replaced by a joiner. The sooner a through-split is caught, the simpler and cheaper the repair.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Rot on a hardwood gate is unusual and indicates either a species that should not have been used (such as untreated softwood), a gate that has been in standing water at the base for extended periods, or a structural joint that has opened and allowed moisture to penetrate the end grain continuously. If rot is present, the affected section needs to be cut out and replaced. A gate specialist or joiner can do this without replacing the entire gate in most cases.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Vehicle damage to a gate leaf (a clipped edge, a dented board) should be repaired promptly because a break in the surface exposes untreated timber to moisture. Sand the damaged area, treat with a preservative end-grain sealer if raw timber is exposed, and re-oil or repaint.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Maintaining the Automation on a Wooden Gate' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If your timber gates are automated, the gate maintenance and the motor maintenance are separate tasks but should be coordinated. Book the [annual motor service](/blog/electric-gate-maintenance-kent/) at the same time as your annual gate inspection. The engineer who services the motor can check the hinge condition, the gate weight (which changes as timber absorbs or releases moisture seasonally), and the motor torque settings at the same time. A timber gate that has absorbed moisture over winter is heavier than the same gate in a dry summer, and the motor torque may need seasonal adjustment on some systems.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Getting Professional Help With Timber Gate Maintenance' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If your gates need more than an oil treatment and a visual check, or if you would rather have a professional handle the annual maintenance, the gate specialists in our Kent network offer service packages that cover both the automation and the gate structure. Submit your details and we will connect you with an installer who covers your area.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};


const article21: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'coastal-gate-corrosion-protection',
  title: 'Do Metal Driveway Gates Rust by the Sea? Coastal Corrosion Protection in Kent',
  metaTitle: 'Do Metal Driveway Gates Rust by the Sea? Kent Coastal Guide',
  metaDescription: 'Salt air on the Kent coast attacks metal gates faster than inland. The galvanising, powder coat, aluminium and stainless specs that actually resist corrosion.',
  category: 'Materials',
  publishDate: '2026-06-23',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-split-wrought-iron-vs-aluminium-sliding.png',
  excerpt: 'Salt air on the Kent coast attacks metal gates faster than inland. The galvanising, powder coat, marine-grade aluminium and stainless specs that actually resist corrosion, from Whitstable to the Romney Marsh edge.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: `If your home sits within sight or smell of the water, from Whitstable round to Dymchurch and the edge of Romney Marsh, you have probably watched cheaper metalwork in the area go from crisp to crumbling in a handful of seasons. Gate hinges weep orange. Powder coat bubbles and flakes. Render-style finishes lift at the corners. The honest answer to the question in the title is yes, ordinary metal gates do rust by the sea, and they rust noticeably faster than the same gate fitted ten miles inland. The useful answer is that this is a specification problem, not a fact of life. The right base metal, the right coating, and the right fixings will hold up against Kent salt air for decades. This guide explains why the coast is so hard on metal and walks through the specs that resist it, so that you can brief any installer with confidence rather than taking their word for what will last.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Why salt air on the Kent coast attacks metal faster' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Corrosion is an electrochemical process. Steel wants to return to iron oxide, and it needs moisture and oxygen to get there. Salt is the accelerant. Airborne chloride from sea spray settles on every surface, and chloride is hygroscopic, meaning it pulls moisture out of the air and holds a thin damp film against the metal even on a dry day. That film conducts the tiny electrical currents that drive rust, so the reaction never really stops. The closer you are to breaking surf and the more exposed you are to onshore wind, the higher the chloride load, which is why a gate in a sheltered courtyard a mile inland in Canterbury behaves quite differently from one facing the open Channel at Folkestone.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Kent's coastline makes this concrete. The north Kent shore around Whitstable, Herne Bay and the Faversham creeks sees steady salt-laden wind off the Thames estuary. Thanet, the most exposed corner of the county, takes weather from three sides at once across Margate, Broadstairs and Ramsgate, with very little shelter for a front-garden gate. Folkestone and Hythe face the Channel directly, and the low, open ground along the Romney Marsh edge offers nothing to slow the wind before it reaches your boundary. Corrosion engineers grade environments like these on a recognised atmospheric scale, and exposed UK coastlines routinely fall into the higher corrosivity bands rather than the gentle category that most inland properties enjoy. The [Galvanizers Association corrosion map](https://galvanizing.org.uk/corrosion/corrosion-map/) lets you look up the measured atmospheric corrosion rate for a specific location on a ten-kilometre grid, and the difference between a coastal Thanet postcode and a sheltered Wealden one is not subtle.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Two further Kent factors make it worse. Wind-driven rain and spray reach the back faces and undersides of a gate that you would never think to inspect, so corrosion often starts where you cannot see it. And salt does not wash off cleanly the way road grime does. It accumulates in the joints, the welds and the bottom rails where water sits, which is exactly why coastal gates tend to fail first at the hinges, the latch and the lowest horizontal member rather than across the flat panels.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Hot-dip galvanising: the foundation for steel by the sea' },
    { type: 'p', text: `If you want a steel gate to survive on the Kent coast, the single most important decision is hot-dip galvanising, and it has to be done properly. This is not the thin, sprayed-on zinc finish that some budget fabricators call galvanised. True hot-dip galvanising means the fully welded gate is dipped in a bath of molten zinc at around 450 degrees, so the zinc metallurgically bonds to the steel and coats every surface, inside and out, including the insides of hollow sections and the depths of every weld.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `That coating protects in two ways at once. It is a physical barrier between the steel and the salt air, and it is sacrificial, so where the surface is scratched or drilled the surrounding zinc corrodes in preference to the steel and shields the exposed area rather than letting rust creep underneath. That galvanic action is why a galvanised gate does not get the spreading under-film rust that ruins a painted-only gate the moment the finish is chipped. According to the Galvanizers Association, a typical coating thickness still delivers a long maintenance-free life even in coastal conditions, which is precisely the margin you want when the salt load is high.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `For a coastal commission the things to confirm are that the steel is galvanised after fabrication rather than assembled from pre-galvanised sheet that leaves cut edges bare, and that the coating thickness suits an exposed marine setting rather than a sheltered inland one. A gate that is welded, then galvanised, then powder coated is the gold-standard build for somewhere like Birchington or Hythe, and it is the approach we steer people toward for any exposed seafront plot. The detail of how galvanising and a colour finish are combined is something we cover when we talk through [our metal driveway gates](/services/metal-driveway-gates/), because the order of operations matters as much as the materials.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Marine-grade powder coat: colour that survives the chloride' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Most homeowners want a colour, not bare grey zinc, and powder coating is how you get a durable one. But not all powder coat is equal, and the marketing word to look for is the architectural and marine-grade specification rather than a standard interior-quality polyester. Coastal powder coating is usually applied as a two-stage system: a zinc-rich or epoxy primer coat that bonds to the galvanised steel and resists undercreep, followed by a tough polyester or super-durable topcoat that carries the colour and takes the UV.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `The reason the duplex approach, galvanising plus powder coat together, outperforms either finish alone is that the two protect each other. The powder coat keeps moisture and chloride away from the zinc so the zinc lasts longer, and the zinc underneath stops any rust spreading if the powder coat is ever chipped at a hinge or by a stray wheel. On the coast this combination roughly multiplies the life you would get from one layer on its own, which is why it is the sensible default from the Whitstable harbour streets round to the Romney Marsh edge. Specify a marine-grade system, insist on the primer stage, and check that the warranty wording actually mentions coastal or marine exposure rather than excluding it, because a finish guaranteed only inland tells you exactly where it expects to fail.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Marine-grade aluminium: skip the rust problem entirely' },
    { type: 'image', src: '/images/gates/gate-aluminium-sliding-horizontal-modern-new-build.png', alt: 'A marine-grade aluminium sliding driveway gate, which does not rust in exposed coastal Kent conditions' },
    { type: 'p', text: `There is a way to sidestep the iron-and-oxygen problem altogether, and that is to choose a gate that does not contain iron in the first place. Marine-grade aluminium does not rust because it has no iron to oxidise. It does form a thin, self-renewing oxide layer the moment it meets air, and that layer is stable and protective rather than flaky like rust, so the metal underneath is sealed from further attack. For an exposed Thanet or Folkestone frontage this is a genuinely strong option, and it is why aluminium is so common in actual marine fittings.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Aluminium gates are also lighter than steel, which is kinder to the automation gear and the posts over a long service life, and they take a powder coat beautifully for colour. The trade-offs to weigh are that aluminium is generally not as strong as steel for a given section, so a very wide or very tall gate may need heavier framing, and that the alloy grade and the fixings genuinely matter on the coast. Pair aluminium with the wrong fasteners and you create a galvanic couple where the steel bolt corrodes aggressively against the aluminium, undoing the whole benefit. Get the alloy and the fixings right and an aluminium gate is close to fit-and-forget by Kent seafront standards, which makes it a frequent recommendation for the most exposed plots around Margate and Hythe.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Stainless fixings: the detail that quietly decides the outcome' },
    { type: 'p', text: `It is worth dwelling on fixings because they are where coastal gates most often fail, and where the cheapest corners are cut. A beautifully galvanised and powder-coated gate hung on ordinary zinc-plated steel bolts will still stain, seize and weep rust at every fixing within a couple of salty winters, because zinc plating is a thin sacrificial layer that the chloride eats through quickly. The hinges, the bolts, the latch components and the post fixings all need to be specified for the environment, not left as an afterthought.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `The right answer near the sea is marine-grade stainless steel, generally the A4 grade, which contains molybdenum specifically to resist chloride pitting that would mark ordinary A2 stainless. Stainless fixings will not bleed rust stains down a pale powder-coated gate, and they keep the moving parts free rather than letting a hinge gradually rust solid. When you brief an installer, ask explicitly what grade of stainless the hinges and fixings are, and treat a vague answer as a warning sign. This is exactly the kind of specification we pin down before recommending any gate for a coastal address, whether that is in [Whitstable](/location/whitstable/), [Folkestone](/location/folkestone/) or anywhere along the exposed stretches, because it is the difference between a gate that ages gracefully and one that looks tired in three years.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Accoya and hardwood: the non-metal route that does not rust at all' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Some homeowners decide the cleanest way to avoid corrosion is to avoid most of the metal, and a timber gate is a legitimate coastal choice when the right wood is used. The standout option is Accoya, a modified softwood that is acetylated through its full thickness so it behaves like a stable, extremely durable hardwood. Because the modification is right through the timber rather than a surface treatment, it does not corrode, it shrugs off salt, and it stays dimensionally stable so it does not swell, stick or warp through the wet coastal winters that make ordinary softwood gates bind in their frames. The Accoya manufacturer notes that the material is built to endure [coastal salt spray and harsh exterior conditions](https://www.accoya.com/wood-siding-extreme-climates/) without losing its integrity, and it carries a long warranted service life.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `A quality hardwood such as oak or iroko is the other timber route and looks superb, though it will ask for more regular oiling than Accoya to hold its finish against the salt. Either way the thing to remember is that a timber gate is not entirely metal-free: it still hangs on hinges and uses fixings and very often pairs with an automation system, so the stainless-fixing rule applies just as firmly here. A timber gate with rusty ironmongery has only moved the corrosion problem, not solved it. Done correctly, timber sits very naturally against the weatherboarded and shingle-clad coastal vernacular you see around the Thanet villages and the Whitstable conservation streets, and the choice between [a wooden gate](/services/wooden-driveway-gates/) and a finished metal one comes down as much to the look you want as to the engineering.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Maintenance schedules that actually matter on the coast' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Even the best coastal specification benefits from a little upkeep, and on the coast the most valuable maintenance job is also the simplest. Rinse the gate down with fresh water every few weeks, more often in winter and after a storm, to wash off the accumulated salt before it can sit and work on the finish. This single habit does more for the life of a coastal gate than anything else, because it removes the chloride that drives the whole process. A bucket and a sponge on the joints, the bottom rail and the hinges is enough; a hose for the panels is a bonus.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `Beyond the rinse, keep a seasonal eye on the parts that fail first. Twice a year, ideally going into and coming out of winter, check the hinges and latch for stiffness and lubricate the moving parts with a marine-suitable grease, inspect the lowest rail and the welds where water collects, and look over the powder coat for any chips that should be touched in promptly before salt finds the steel underneath. Galvanised and duplex-coated steel and marine aluminium need very little beyond this. A timber gate adds a re-oiling or recoating cycle on top, more often for hardwood than for Accoya. If the gate is automated, have the mechanism and its electrics checked on the same rhythm, since the same salt air works on motors, hinges and circuit boards, and that wider servicing is part of what [gate repair and maintenance](/services/gate-repair-and-maintenance/) is for along this coast.` },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Briefing an installer with confidence' },
    { type: 'p', text: `Pulling the threads together, a metal driveway gate that lasts on the Kent coast is not a single product but a stack of correct decisions: a base material that suits the exposure, either galvanised steel or marine-grade aluminium, a duplex finish where steel is used, marine-grade A4 stainless for every hinge and fixing, and a realistic rinse-and-inspect routine once it is installed. Get those right and the sea stops being a threat to your gate. Get any one of them wrong, most often the fixings, and the coast will find the weak point within a couple of winters.` },
    { type: 'p', text: `If you are weighing up a new gate for a coastal property anywhere from the north Kent shore through Thanet to the Romney Marsh edge, tell us the address, the exposure and the look you are after, and use the form on this page to request quotes from local installers who build to a genuine coastal specification rather than an inland one. You will get back proposals you can compare on the things that actually decide longevity by the sea.` },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};


const article22: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'pedestrian-gate-alongside-driveway-gates-kent',
  title: 'Adding a Pedestrian Gate Alongside Your Driveway Gates in Kent',
  metaTitle: 'Pedestrian Gate Alongside Driveway Gates Kent | 2026 Guide',
  metaDescription: 'Why fit a matching pedestrian gate next to your driveway gates in Kent. Materials, lock and access options, post and delivery access, safety, and rough cost considerations.',
  category: 'Buying Guide',
  publishDate: '2026-06-23',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-wrought-iron-open-stone-pillars-lanterns-estate.png',
  excerpt: 'A separate pedestrian gate keeps people on foot away from the swing or slide path of the main gates and saves you cycling the motor every time someone walks in. Here is how to plan one for a Kent property.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'Most Kent homeowners plan their driveway gates around the car and only think about people on foot once the gates are in. A matching pedestrian gate, sometimes called a side gate or wicket gate, solves a set of everyday problems that a single vehicle entrance cannot. It gives visitors, postal staff, and family members a way in and out without operating the main gates, and it keeps anyone on foot clear of a moving swing leaf or sliding panel. On a busy household it quickly proves its worth, and it is far cheaper to design in at the start than to add later.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Why a Separate Pedestrian Gate Is Worth Fitting' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Cycling an automated gate open and shut for a single person walking in puts unnecessary cycles on the motor and slows everyone down. A pedestrian gate lets people pass through directly while the driveway gates stay closed and secure. It also removes a genuine safety concern, because people no longer need to walk through the arc of a swing gate or alongside the path of a sliding gate. For families with children or pets, having a defined walk-through entrance that does not involve the powered gate is a sensible separation of foot traffic from vehicle traffic.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'There is a [security benefit](/blog/driveway-gates-home-security-kent/) too. If the pedestrian gate carries its own lock, you can let someone in on foot without leaving the main vehicle entrance standing open. That matters on properties where the driveway gates take several seconds to travel and would otherwise sit open while a visitor crosses the threshold.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Matching Materials and Finish to Your Driveway Gates' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A pedestrian gate looks best when it reads as part of the same set rather than an afterthought. The usual approach is to build it in the [same material](/blog/best-gate-material-kent-wood-steel-aluminium/), infill style, and colour as the driveway gates, scaled down to a single leaf around 900mm to 1000mm wide. If your main gates are powder-coated steel in anthracite, the side gate should match that exact finish. If they are hardwood, the side gate should use the same timber and the same close-boarded or palisade pattern.' },
    { type: 'list', items: [
      'Match the infill style so vertical bars, close-boarding, or panel design carries across both gates',
      'Specify the identical paint or powder-coat colour, and for coastal Kent properties the same marine-grade finish',
      'Keep the top profile consistent, whether flat-topped, arched, or with a decorative head',
      'Set the pedestrian gate height to align with the driveway gates rather than standing noticeably taller or shorter',
    ]},
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Lock and Access Options' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A pedestrian gate gives you more lock choices than an automated vehicle gate. A simple mechanical latch with a thumb-turn on the inside suits households that mainly want to keep the gate closed against pets and wind. For controlled access, a keypad lock or an electric strike released by an intercom lets you admit visitors from inside the house. On installations that already include a video intercom for the driveway gates, it is common to wire the pedestrian gate release into the same system so a single panel controls both.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If you want the pedestrian gate to fail safe in a power cut, discuss this with your installer, because the choice between a fail-open and fail-locked release affects both security and how you get in and out when the electricity is off. A mechanical key override on the lock is a sensible backup whichever electric option you choose.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Access for Post and Deliveries' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Deliveries are the single most common reason Kent households add a pedestrian gate. Couriers and postal staff need a reliable way to reach the door without you having to open the main gates remotely each time. A keypad or a simple latch that a delivery driver can operate, combined with clear signage, keeps parcels coming to the door rather than being left at the gate or taken away. Some owners fit a secure parcel box just inside the pedestrian gate so deliveries can be left safely even when nobody is home. Plan the path from the pedestrian gate to the front door at the same time, since a level, well-lit route matters as much as the gate itself.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Safety and Standards' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A manually operated pedestrian gate is straightforward, but it should still be hung on robust hinges, fitted with a self-closing latch where children or pets are a concern, and positioned so it does not open into the path of the moving driveway gates. Where the pedestrian gate is automated or power-assisted, the same [force-limitation and safety principles that govern powered driveway gates](https://www.hse.gov.uk/work-equipment-machinery/electric-gates.htm) apply, and the installation should be [commissioned to the recognised standard for powered gates](https://www.gatesafe.org/). A [reputable installer](/blog/choosing-gate-installer-kent/) will assess crushing and shearing points and confirm the gate is safe before handover.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Rough Cost Considerations' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A matching manual pedestrian gate is a modest addition relative to the driveway gates themselves, and the cost depends mainly on the material, the finish, and whether you add electric access control. As an indicative guide only, a simple matching side gate with a mechanical latch sits at the lower end, while a powder-coated or hardwood gate with a keypad or intercom-released lock costs more once wiring and access hardware are included. Treat any figure you see online as a starting point and get a site-specific quote, because pillar work, ground conditions, and the lock specification all move the price.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};


const article23: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'off-grid-power-for-gates-on-rural-kent-farm-drives',
  title: 'Off-Grid Power for Gates on Long Rural Kent Farm Drives',
  metaTitle: 'Off-Grid Solar Gate Power for Rural Kent Farm Drives | Guide',
  metaDescription: 'No mains at the gate down a long Kent farm or orchard drive? How solar and battery power, mains trenching, and battery backup compare for working land in the Weald, Marsh and orchard belt.',
  category: 'Automation',
  publishDate: '2026-07-02',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-opening-driver-pov-tree-lined-driveway.png',
  excerpt: 'Working farms, orchards and Marsh smallholdings often put the gate hundreds of metres from the nearest mains. Here is how solar, trenched mains and battery backup really compare on rural Kent land.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'A great many rural Kent properties put the gate a long way from any usable power. A fruit farm off a track near Faversham, a converted oast up an unmade lane in the Weald around Cranbrook, a Marsh smallholding on the flat land behind Dymchurch: the farmhouse has mains, but the point where you actually want the automated gate is fifty, a hundred, sometimes two hundred metres away with only orchard, hedge and verge in between. The gate and the motor are the easy part. Powering the thing reliably down a working agricultural drive is the decision that shapes the whole job, and it is where Kent land behaves differently from a suburban close.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'There are three honest routes: trench a mains spur out to the gate, run the gate off solar with a battery, or accept mains at the gate but design in battery backup for the power cuts that are a fact of life on rural feeders. Which one fits depends on the length of the run, the ground it crosses, how hard the gate will be worked, and whether the plot sits under a planning designation that limits what you can dig.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Why a farm drive is not a suburban drive' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'On a working holding the gate earns its keep in a way a residential gate does not. It opens for the feed lorry, the fruit pickers arriving at dawn in the orchard belt around Faversham and Teynham, contractors with trailers and the family cars, then closes behind each one. That duty cycle matters, because every power option is really a question of how many open-and-close operations you can sustain on a wet week in December. A gate that cycles a handful of times a day is a very different sizing problem from one on a busy farmyard entrance, and getting that count honest at the survey is what separates a system that works from one that flattens its battery by teatime. The ground is different too: much of the Weald sits on heavy clay that moves when wet and sets hard when dry, the Marsh is low and prone to a high water table, and orchard land is criss-crossed with roots you do not want to cut through. All of that pushes on the trenching option, and it is part of why so many rural Kent gates end up on solar even where mains looked cheaper on paper.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Solar and battery: the usual answer on a long Kent drive' },
    { type: 'image', src: '/images/gates/gate-wooden-iroko-closeup-kent-weald-countryside.png', alt: 'A rural driveway gate on Kent Weald farmland where solar power avoids a long mains trench' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Where the run is genuinely long or the ground is awkward, a self-contained solar setup is usually the better economic call. A panel mounted on or beside the gate pillar charges a deep-cycle battery, and the battery drives a low-current motor through its daily cycles with no trench, no armoured cable and no reinstatement of the drive. For a quiet domestic-style gate down a farm track it is often not close: solar wins on cost and on the sheer avoidance of disruption to working land.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The honest part is the sizing. Kit sold for sunny climates does not reliably run a gate through a grey Kent winter, so the system has to be specified around December daylight rather than a brochure photograph. As a rough shape of the numbers, a modest panel of around 20 watts paired with a small deep-cycle battery can be enough for a gate cycling up to roughly ten times a day, while a harder-worked farm gate wants more panel and more storage, typically a 30 watt or larger monocrystalline panel and one or two 12 volt deep-cycle batteries wired to suit a 12 or 24 volt motor. Treat those as indicative starting points, not a shopping list, because duty cycle and site aspect move them a long way. The general principle of pairing generation with the right size of store is the same one the Energy Saving Trust sets out for [home solar and battery storage](https://energysavingtrust.org.uk/advice/battery-storage/), scaled right down to a single gate.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The two things that quietly undermine a solar gate are shade and appetite for power. A panel behind an orchard row, a tall Wealden hedge or a stand of trees will never see enough winter sun, so the position needs settling at survey rather than after. And solar runs a motor happily, but hang a power-hungry video intercom, floodlighting and a heated lock off the same small battery and you are asking too much of it. That is the point where a mains spur quietly wins back the argument on capability, even though it costs more to put in.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Trenching a mains spur: robust, but the ground decides the cost' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The most capable answer is still a buried mains spur from the farmhouse consumer unit out to the gate, terminating in a weatherproof enclosure with its own protection. Armoured cable laid at the correct depth and ducted where vehicles and heavy plant cross gives the motor a steady supply and leaves headroom to add an intercom, lighting and a camera later without rethinking the power. On a busy farm entrance that flexibility is worth a lot.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The catch is cost, and it scales with distance and with the Kent ground. A short hop is trivial. A hundred-metre-plus run across a paddock or an orchard means a real length of cable, a trencher on hire, careful routing around tree roots and field drains, and reinstatement of anything you cut through. Over long distances voltage drop stops being a footnote and becomes a design factor, so the cable is sized up rather than run at the minimum gauge. It is the line item that ambushes people who priced the gate and motor first.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A middle route exists for the awkward drives that suit neither pure mains nor pure solar. A low-voltage motor fed from a transformer near the house will run further on a thinner cable than full mains, and some installs pair a modest solar panel with a trickle mains feed so the battery never runs flat in a dark spell. These hybrids are common on the longer Kent farm drives, and they are worth raising early so the motor and controller are chosen to match. The trade-offs sit alongside the rest of what goes into an [automated gate system](/services/automated-gate-systems/), because the power method dictates which motor and accessories are realistic in the first place.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Battery backup and what happens in a power cut' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Rural Kent feeders drop out more than urban ones, and a gate you cannot open when the electricity is off is worse than a manual gate. Whichever power route you choose, the sensible specification includes battery backup and a manual release you can actually reach. A solar gate already runs from a battery, so it rides through a grid outage without noticing. A mains gate should carry a backup battery in the control box sized to give you a useful number of operations, so the gate still opens for the school run or an ambulance when the power is down.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Two practical points matter on a farm. First, decide with your installer whether the gate should fail safe, meaning it can be pushed open by hand when unpowered, or fail secure, and make sure the manual key release is somewhere you can get to it in the dark and the rain rather than buried behind the motor cover. Second, remember that batteries do not like being ignored, and salt-laden coastal air and damp Marsh conditions are hard on electrics, so backup batteries and connections should be on the same checklist as the mechanics. That is exactly the sort of thing routine [gate servicing and maintenance](/blog/electric-gate-maintenance-kent/) is meant to catch before it strands you, and it is doubly worth it near the coast, where the corrosion pressures that drive the [coastal material choices](/blog/coastal-gate-corrosion-protection/) work on the motor and its wiring too.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Security on a remote farm gate' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Power and security are the same conversation on isolated land. Farm, plant and vehicle theft is a real pressure across rural Kent, and a gate at the end of a long unlit drive is both the first line of defence and, if it is dead in a power cut, a potential weak point. Designing the power so the gate stays closed and controllable at all hours is part of keeping the holding secure, and it feeds directly into the wider [home and property security](/blog/driveway-gates-home-security-kent/) picture: automatic closing so the gate is never left open by accident, a camera or intercom at the entrance where a connected supply supports it, and anti-lift measures on the hinges of a gate that sits out of sight of the house. On a working farm the gate is not decoration, and the power that keeps it working is part of the security spend, not separate from it.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'The planning angle on rural Kent land' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Power is not only an electrical question on a protected landscape, and much of rural Kent is protected. The Kent Downs and the High Weald are both National Landscapes, formerly Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty, and permitted development rights are tighter inside them and inside conservation areas. The gate itself usually sits within ordinary rules: under the [national permitted development guidance for gates, fences and walls](https://www.planningportal.co.uk/permission/common-projects/fences-gates-and-garden-walls/planning-permission/), a gate next to a highway used by vehicles must not exceed one metre in height without consent, while elsewhere on the boundary the threshold is two metres. Those rights can be removed by an Article 4 direction, a planning condition, or a listed-building or curtilage-listed status, which is common on Kent farmsteads and oast houses.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The trenching is where the designation bites hardest. A buried cable run does not usually trouble a planning office on its own, but a long trench combined with new pillars, hardstanding and lighting on protected land can tip a scheme into territory the district wants to look at, and the 12 Kent district councils and Medway Council each apply their local plan differently. This is one more reason solar appeals on the most sensitive plots: no trench means far less to justify. Before committing to a route it is worth reading the county-specific detail we set out on [planning permission for driveway gates in Kent](/blog/planning-permission-driveway-gates-kent/), because the designation you sit under changes the answer.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Working out the right option for your drive' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The decision comes down to measurements taken on site: the distance from a usable supply to the gate, the surface and the ground the cable would cross, the honest daily open-and-close count, and how much you want to power beyond the motor itself. A short run to a busy gate that wants an intercom and cameras points to trenched mains with battery backup. A long run to a quiet gate down an orchard track points to solar. The awkward middle, which is most rural Kent drives, usually lands on a hybrid that someone has actually sized rather than guessed, and often on an aluminium or duplex-coated gate where the plot is close enough to the coast for salt to be a factor.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If you tell an installer the rough length of your drive, the ground it crosses, how hard the gate will be worked, and whether your land sits in the Kent Downs, the High Weald or near the coast, you will get proposals you can compare on the things that actually decide reliability. Use the form on this page to be matched with local specialists who fit power to long rural drives around the Weald, the orchard belt and the Marsh, from [Cranbrook](/location/cranbrook/) and [Tenterden](/location/tenterden/) to [Faversham](/location/faversham/), rather than treating every gate as if it sat ten metres from the house.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};


const article24: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'listed-building-consent-driveway-gates-kent',
  title: 'Do You Need Listed Building Consent for Driveway Gates in Kent?',
  metaTitle: 'Listed Building Consent for Driveway Gates in Kent | Guide',
  metaDescription: 'Kent has thousands of listed oast houses, farmsteads and barn conversions. When driveway gates need listed building consent, how curtilage listing works, and the approval process.',
  category: 'Planning',
  publishDate: '2026-07-02',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-wooden-oak-open-interior-tree-lined-lane.png',
  draft: false,
  excerpt: 'A driveway gate on a listed Kent property, or even one within its curtilage, can need listed building consent that ordinary permitted development rules never trigger. Here is when consent bites and how the process works.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'Kent carries one of the heaviest concentrations of listed buildings in England, and a great many are not grand houses but working rural stock: oast houses with their brick roundels and white cowls, Wealden hall houses, timber-frame farmhouses, and the barn and cart-lodge conversions that now serve as family homes across the countryside from Cranbrook and Tenterden to the Faversham fruit belt. If your property is listed, or sits within the grounds of a listed building, the rules that govern a new driveway gate are stricter than most homeowners expect, and getting them wrong carries consequences that a normal gate installation never does. When consent bites, how curtilage listing catches gates nowhere near the main house, and how the approval runs are all worth understanding before you order anything.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Permitted development stops at the listed boundary' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'For an ordinary Kent home, a driveway gate is usually straightforward. Under the national rules a gate can go up without a planning application provided it does not exceed one metre in height where it sits next to a highway used by vehicles, or two metres in height elsewhere on the boundary. Those thresholds are set out in the [Planning Portal guidance on fences, gates and garden walls](https://www.planningportal.co.uk/permission/common-projects/fences-gates-and-garden-walls/planning-permission), and for the majority of properties they are the whole story.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Listed status changes that completely. The same guidance is explicit that these permitted development rights do not apply where any part of the site is a listed building or within the curtilage of a listed building, or where the gate would form a boundary with a neighbouring listed building or its curtilage. In other words, the automatic right that lets your neighbour hang a gate without asking anyone simply does not exist for you. That is a separate question from planning permission, and it is easy to conflate the two: you can be well within the height limits, cause no highway issue at all, and still need consent purely because of what your building is. The county-wide picture of National Landscape designations, Green Belt and conservation areas that shapes ordinary applications, covered in the overview of [planning permission for driveway gates in Kent](/blog/planning-permission-driveway-gates-kent/), sits on top of the listed-building question rather than replacing it.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What listed building consent is, and why gates fall inside it' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Listed building consent is the permission you need to alter, extend or demolish a listed building in a way that affects its character as a building of special architectural or historic interest. It is governed by the Planning (Listed Buildings and Conservation Areas) Act 1990, it is separate from planning permission, and it is free to apply for. Crucially, it can be needed for work to the setting and the boundary, not only to the fabric of the house itself, and a new gate at the entrance to a listed property is exactly the kind of change that can fall within its scope, particularly where the gate is fixed to a listed wall or pier, replaces a historic gate, or materially changes how the building is approached and seen.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The reason this matters so much on Kent stock is the variety of listed detail at the boundary. Many of the county oast houses and farmhouses still have their original brick or ragstone piers, staddle stones and old field entrances, and a modern gate bolted onto or set between those features is altering listed fabric even though it feels like a garden job. Historic England, the government body that advises on the historic environment, is the sensible first reference for any owner weighing up a scheme.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Curtilage listing: the trap that catches gates far from the house' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The idea that surprises people most is curtilage listing. When a building is listed, the protection can extend beyond the main structure to other buildings and structures within its curtilage, broadly the land and ancillary buildings that formed part of the property when it was first listed. A cart lodge, a former stable, a garden wall, an old pump house, and in some cases the boundary walls and piers themselves can all be treated as part of the listing even though they are not named on the list entry. On a converted Kent farmstead this is common, because the barns, oasts and outbuildings that now make up the dwellings and their grounds were once the ancillary structures of a single listed farmhouse.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'For gates the practical effect is that consent can bite even when the entrance is a long way from the house, if the piers or walls the gate attaches to are curtilage-listed, or if the new gate changes the setting of a curtilage structure. Whether a particular wall or entrance falls inside the curtilage is a technical judgement that turns on the history and layout of the site, and it is where owners most often assume they are clear when they are not. Historic England sets out how these boundaries are assessed in its advice on [listed buildings and curtilage](https://historicengland.org.uk/images-books/publications/listed-buildings-and-curtilage-advice-note-10/), and where there is any doubt the district conservation officer will give an informed view before you commit to a design.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'The risk of skipping consent' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'This is not a box-ticking formality that you can quietly ignore. Carrying out works to a listed building that need consent without obtaining it first is a criminal offence, not merely a planning breach that can be tidied up later. The offence is a strict one, which means the authority does not have to prove you intended any harm, and not knowing that the building or the entrance was listed is not a defence. Both the owner and anyone who carried out the work, including the installer, can be prosecuted, and the council can serve an enforcement notice requiring an unauthorised gate and any altered pier or wall to be put back as it was, at your cost.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'the 12 Kent district councils and Medway Council each run their own conservation function, but none treats unauthorised work to a listed entrance lightly, and a gate installed without consent can also surface awkwardly at the point of sale when a buyer solicitor asks for paperwork that was never obtained. Establish the position before any material is ordered, because a scheme designed with the conservation officer from the start is far cheaper than one unpicked after a complaint.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Designing a gate that a conservation officer will approve' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Consent is very obtainable when the design respects the building, and Kent conservation officers approve sympathetic new gates on listed properties regularly. The instinct that works is restraint: a gate that reads as though it belongs to the vernacular of the building rather than announcing itself as new. On the timber-frame farmhouses, oast houses and barn conversions of the Weald that usually means hardwood, close-boarded or simply framed, on traditional strap hinges, in oak or a comparable timber that will silver down and settle against aged brick and ragstone. Elaborate scrollwork, turned detail and machine-cut decoration tend to look wrong on agricultural buildings and are the kind of thing an officer will resist. The material and detailing choices that suit these buildings run alongside the design thinking for [older houses and period properties](/blog/driveway-gate-ideas-for-older-houses-and-period-properties/), where the same read-the-building principle applies.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Two further points carry real weight in an application. The first is that automation must be invisible: underground motors are the usual answer on a listed entrance, or a ram-arm concealed on the inner face of the gate and pier, so no motor housing, bracket or control box intrudes on the historic view. The second is that fixing method matters, because how a gate meets a listed pier is itself part of what is being consented, and a reversible fixing that avoids cutting into historic brickwork is easier to justify than one that hacks into it. Where a bespoke [wooden driveway gate](/services/wooden-driveway-gates/) is being made for the property, the joinery and ironmongery can be specified from the outset to match the building, which is the sort of detail that turns a marginal application into an approved one.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'How the application process runs' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The route is broadly the same across Kent, with local variation in how much the officer wants to see up front. A pre-application conversation with the district conservation officer is the sensible first step, and it is often free or low cost. It establishes whether the entrance is listed or curtilage-listed at all and gives you an early steer on what design will be acceptable, which saves you paying for drawings that will not fly. From there a formal listed building consent application goes to the relevant district council, supported by a heritage statement that explains the significance of the building and its setting and how the proposed gate preserves it, along with drawings, materials and finish details.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Where the property also sits in one of the county protected landscapes the two questions run together, because much of the listed rural stock lies inside the Kent Downs or the High Weald, and the [National Landscape planning position across the North Downs and High Weald](/blog/planning-permission-driveway-gates-kent/) will shape the design brief alongside the listing. A single submission that addresses both the heritage significance and the landscape setting is far stronger than treating them as separate afterthoughts. Timescales vary by district and by the complexity of the building, so start early rather than assuming a gate can be turned around in a few weeks.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Getting the right people on a listed Kent entrance' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A listed property is not the job for a general fencing contractor. The installers who succeed on these entrances are the ones who have designed to a conservation officer before, who can produce a gate and ironmongery that suit a timber-frame or oast property, and who understand that a reversible, sympathetic fixing to a historic pier is part of the specification rather than an inconvenience. If your Kent property is listed, is a barn or oast conversion, or you simply suspect the piers at your entrance might be caught by a nearby listing, tell us the address and what you know of its history, and use the form on this page to be matched with local specialists who work on listed and curtilage-listed properties and can guide the consent process rather than leaving you to navigate it alone.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};


const article25: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'best-timber-for-a-coastal-driveway-gate-kent',
  title: 'The Best Timber for a Coastal Driveway Gate in Kent',
  metaTitle: 'Best Timber for a Coastal Gate in Kent | Accoya and Iroko',
  metaDescription: 'Which timber lasts on a salt-exposed Kent coast gate? Accoya, iroko and oak compared for the Whitstable, Thanet and Folkestone belt, plus fixings, finish and silvering.',
  category: 'Materials',
  publishDate: '2026-07-02',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-wooden-painted-cream-kentish-countryside.png',
  draft: true,
  excerpt: 'A wooden gate on the Kent coast lives with salt, wind and constant wetting and drying that a sheltered inland gate never sees. The timber you pick decides whether it lasts five years or fifty. Here is how Accoya, iroko and oak really compare on a seafront plot.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'A timber gate on the open Kent coast has a hard life. The band from Whitstable round Thanet to Folkestone and Hythe takes salt-laden onshore wind, driving rain, and a daily cycle of wetting and drying that works joints loose and lifts paint far faster than anything a sheltered Wealden lane throws at a gate. Homeowners on the seafront often assume the answer is simply a dense tropical hardwood, but the real question is dimensional stability as much as raw durability, because a gate that swells and shrinks with every damp spell will rack, bind on its hinges and split its finish whatever species it is cut from. Getting the timber right at the outset is the single biggest thing you can do to keep a coastal gate looking and working well.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'There is a genuine spread between the sensible options, and it is not always the most expensive timber that wins on the coast. The three worth weighing up for a Kent seafront gate are acetylated softwood such as Accoya, a durable tropical hardwood such as iroko, and traditional European oak, and each behaves differently once salt and wind are in the picture.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Why the coast is harder on timber than the Weald' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Inland, a hardwood gate mostly has to resist rot and the slow greying of the sun. On the coast it also has to cope with airborne salt that holds moisture against the surface, wind that dries the exposed face while the sheltered face stays damp, and far more frequent rain-then-sun cycling. That uneven moisture movement is what pulls a gate out of square over time. Salt does not rot timber the way it corrodes steel, but it keeps the surface wet for longer, and prolonged wetting is exactly what fungal decay needs, so a species that would be perfectly durable two miles inland can fail early right on the front. The finish suffers first: paint and stain crack along the grain where the wood moves underneath them, then let water in behind, and once that starts the gate is on a downward path. This is why the [maintenance a wooden gate needs](/blog/how-to-look-after-wooden-driveway-gates/) is heavier on the coast, and why the timber choice matters more here than anywhere else in the county.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Accoya: the stability specialist for exposed fronts' },
    { type: 'image', src: '/images/gates/gate-wooden-oak-open-interior-tree-lined-lane.png', alt: 'A hardwood driveway gate of the kind used on exposed Kent coastal plots' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Accoya is a modified softwood, usually radiata pine, put through an acetylation process that changes the wood so it barely takes up water. That matters enormously on the coast, because a timber that hardly swells or shrinks holds its shape and holds a paint film through the constant damp cycling that destroys ordinary joinery. It reaches the top natural durability rating, Class 1 under EN 350, which is equal to or better than teak, and the manufacturer covers above-ground use with a fifty year warranty that explicitly extends to salt-water splash zones. The technical picture is set out in the [Accoya wood information guide](https://www.accoya.com/app/uploads/2020/04/Accoya_WoodInfoGuide-1.pdf), and for a fully exposed seafront gate it is often the most rational choice despite being a softwood, precisely because the failure mode on the coast is movement and finish breakdown rather than rot alone. It takes paint and colour beautifully, which suits a painted coastal look, and if left unpainted it silvers to an even grey within a year or two.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Iroko and oak: the hardwood options' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Iroko, sometimes sold as African teak, is a genuine coastal performer. It carries natural oils that resist moisture and fungal attack without treatment and reaches Class 1 to 2 durability, so it stands up to salt exposure well and can be left to weather to silver-grey or oiled to keep its warm tone. The trade-offs are practical: its interlocked grain makes it harder to machine cleanly, and those same oils can fight paint adhesion, so it is best specified with an experienced joiner and finished with oil rather than paint. European oak is the traditional Kent estate timber and lasts decades when detailed well, but it moves more than Accoya and stains iron-grey where it meets steel fixings in damp salt air, so on a fully exposed front it needs more careful design than in a sheltered spot. A neutral overview of how these species and modified timbers compare on durability class is set out by [ForestSource on modified and durable wood](https://forestsource.io/topics/modified-wood), which is a useful sense-check against any single supplier claim. The broader [wood versus metal decision for a Kent gate](/blog/best-gate-material-kent-wood-steel-aluminium/) also sits behind this, because on the most brutal seafront plots some owners conclude that a marine-grade metal gate is the lower-maintenance answer.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Cost tends to sort these options in a way that surprises people. Accoya usually sits above ordinary softwood and can approach hardwood prices, which feels counter-intuitive for a modified pine until the warranty and the low movement are weighed in. Iroko is a genuine hardwood price, and good oak is dearer still for a well-made estate gate. On the coast the sensible way to read those numbers is over the life of the gate rather than at the point of purchase, because a cheaper timber that needs replacing or heavily reworking after a handful of hard winters is no saving at all. Properly maintained, oak, iroko and Accoya can all give forty to sixty years or more, but that word maintained does a lot of work on an exposed front, and the timber that holds its finish and its shape with the least intervention is usually the one that proves cheapest in the end. Treat any price you see as an indicative range and get a coastal-specific quote, since exposure, size and finish move it considerably.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Fixings and finish matter as much as the species' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A coastal timber gate is only as good as its ironmongery. Ordinary or even standard stainless fixings can bleed and corrode in salt air, so hinges, bolts and screws should be marine-grade stainless steel, typically the 316 grade specified for coastal work, or hot-dip galvanised and then coated. The same salt that corrodes an all-metal gate works on the ironmongery of a timber one, so the metal parts of a coastal timber gate deserve the same anti-corrosion care as the gate itself. On the finish, a microporous coating that flexes with the wood outlasts a hard film that cracks, and whatever you choose will need recoating more often facing the sea than it would inland. Detailing the gate to shed water quickly, with weathered top rails and no flat surfaces that pond, does as much for its life as the timber grade.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'What this means for a Kent seafront gate' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'If your gate faces open water anywhere along the Whitstable to Folkestone belt, prioritise stability and durability class over the prestige of a name. Accoya is hard to beat where the gate is painted and fully exposed, iroko suits an oiled hardwood look in experienced hands, and oak remains a fine choice on a slightly more sheltered coastal plot detailed with care. Whichever timber you pick, insist on marine-grade fixings and a flexible finish, and have the gate made to a coastal specification rather than a standard inland one. If you want a [bespoke wooden gate](/services/wooden-driveway-gates/) built for a salt-exposed Kent front, tell us where the plot sits and how exposed it is, and use the form on this page to be matched with local makers who specify timber and ironmongery for the coast rather than for a sheltered close.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};


const article26: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'aluminium-vs-steel-driveway-gates-for-kent-seafront-plots',
  title: 'Aluminium vs Steel Driveway Gates for Kent Seafront Plots',
  metaTitle: 'Aluminium vs Steel Gates for Kent Seafront Plots | Guide',
  metaDescription: 'On a salt-exposed Kent seafront, does aluminium or galvanised steel last longer? A straight comparison of corrosion, weight, cost and finish for coastal metal driveway gates.',
  category: 'Materials',
  publishDate: '2026-07-02',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-aluminium-sliding-horizontal-modern-new-build.png',
  draft: true,
  excerpt: 'For a metal gate right on the Kent coast the real fight is aluminium against galvanised steel, and salt tilts it hard one way. Here is how the two compare on corrosion, weight, cost and finish for a seafront plot.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'Once a Kent seafront owner has decided on a metal gate rather than timber, the choice narrows to two serious contenders: marine-grade aluminium or galvanised and coated steel. Both can be made to last on the coast, but they do not last in the same way or for the same reasons, and the salt-laden air off the Channel and the Thames Estuary pushes the decision much harder than it would inland. Getting it right is the difference between a gate that shrugs off the sea for decades and one that streaks with rust or blisters its coating within a few winters. The honest comparison comes down to how each metal deals with salt, how heavy it is on the automation, what finish it holds, and what it costs.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'This is a coastal-specific question. Two miles inland the difference between the two metals is minor and mostly about weight and style. On an exposed front at Whitstable, Herne Bay, Tankerton or the Thanet and Folkestone seafronts, the corrosion behaviour becomes the whole argument, and it is worth understanding before you commit either way.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Corrosion: the argument the coast settles' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Aluminium does not rust. It forms a thin, self-healing oxide layer within milliseconds of exposure to air, and that layer is what protects it in salt conditions. Marine alloys in the 5000 series, alloyed with magnesium, are specified precisely because they hold up in seawater, losing only a fraction of the material that ordinary aluminium would in the same conditions. The 5000-series properties that make them the coastal standard are set out by [1st Choice Metals on 5000-series aluminium](https://www.1stchoicemetals.co.uk/aluminium/grades-guide/5000-series/). Galvanised steel is protected differently: a zinc coating sacrifices itself to protect the steel, but in a marine atmosphere the zinc is consumed faster. Under the recognised corrosivity categories a salt-exposed coast is the very high C5 class, where hot-dip galvanising loses its coating far quicker than in a mild inland setting, and splash zones are harder still. The corrosion rates for each category are documented by the [American Galvanizers Association on coastal climates](https://galvanizeit.org/knowledgebase/article/hdg-in-coastal-climates), and they show why a steel gate on the front needs a heavier duplex system of galvanising plus a quality powder coat, and ongoing attention, to match what aluminium does more or less on its own. This is the same salt battle that shapes every [coastal corrosion decision on a Kent gate](/blog/coastal-gate-corrosion-protection/), just seen through the narrower lens of two metals head to head.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'It is worth being clear about what galvanising does and does not do, because the word gets used loosely. Hot-dip galvanising immerses the finished steel in molten zinc so the whole surface, inside and out, is coated, and the zinc then corrodes in preference to the steel. That is real protection and it is far better than paint alone, but it is a consumable barrier: the zinc is spent over time, and on a salt-battered front it is spent faster. Pairing galvanising with a good powder coat, the duplex system, slows the process because the paint protects the zinc and the zinc protects the steel, and the two together last longer than the sum of their separate lives. The catch is that any coating is only as good as its weakest point, so a chip, a drilled hole for a lock, or a weld that was not properly re-protected becomes the place where rust starts. That is why a coastal steel gate rewards a builder who understands the specification and an owner who touches up damage promptly, whereas an aluminium gate is far more forgiving of a knock because there is no sacrificial layer to breach in the first place.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Weight, automation and everyday running' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Aluminium is far lighter than steel for the same gate, and on the coast that has knock-on benefits. A lighter leaf puts less strain on the motor, the hinges and the pillars, so the automation works less hard and the parts that do wear are under less load. On a windy seafront, where the gate is cycling in gusts, that lighter mass is easier on the whole system. Steel is heavier and stronger, which some owners value for security and for a solid feel, but the weight demands more robust pillars and a beefier motor, and every one of those steel components is itself exposed to the same salt. The result is that an aluminium coastal gate tends to be simpler to keep running, while a steel one rewards more diligent servicing. Whichever you choose, the salt air works on the motor, the lock and the wiring as well as the gate, so a proper [annual gate service on the coast](/blog/electric-gate-maintenance-kent/) is not optional; it is what stops small corrosion problems becoming a seized gate.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Finish, style and cost' },
    { type: 'image', src: '/images/gates/gate-split-wrought-iron-vs-aluminium-sliding.png', alt: 'A wrought-iron style steel gate next to a modern aluminium sliding gate, the two coastal metal options for a Kent seafront' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Both metals take a powder-coat finish in any colour, so the aesthetic is not the deciding factor it once was. Steel still owns the traditional wrought-iron look, with scrolls, spear tops and the heft that suits a period coastal villa, while aluminium suits clean, contemporary horizontal-slat and flat-panel designs that read well against a modern seafront build. On cost, aluminium usually carries a higher up-front price for the gate itself, but that has to be weighed against a steel gate on the coast needing a more elaborate protective system and more frequent recoating over its life. The sensible way to read the numbers is over the whole life of the gate rather than on the initial quote alone, and treat any figure as an indicative range that a site survey will firm up. The wider three-way view of [wood, steel and aluminium for a Kent gate](/blog/best-gate-material-kent-wood-steel-aluminium/) puts this metal choice in context, because for some seafront owners the honest answer is a marine-grade aluminium gate that they can largely forget about.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Picking the right metal for your front' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'For a fully exposed Kent seafront plot, marine-grade aluminium is the lower-maintenance answer and usually the one that ages best with the least attention, which is why it dominates modern coastal installs. Galvanised and duplex-coated steel remains a strong choice where you want a traditional heavy wrought-iron look or maximum physical strength, provided you accept that it needs a proper coastal coating system and regular upkeep to earn its keep by the sea. The one thing to avoid is a standard inland steel gate simply painted and hoped for; that is the specification that streaks and fails first on the front. If you are choosing a [metal driveway gate](/services/metal-driveway-gates/) for a salt-exposed Kent plot, tell us how close to open water it sits and which look you want, and use the form on this page to be matched with installers who specify to a genuine coastal standard.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};


const article27: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'five-bar-and-estate-field-gates-for-rural-kent-drives',
  title: 'Five-Bar and Estate Field Gates for Rural Kent Drives',
  metaTitle: 'Five-Bar and Estate Field Gates for Rural Kent Drives | Guide',
  metaDescription: 'When a five-bar field gate or estate gate suits a rural Kent farm or paddock drive. Timber and metal options, widths, automation and the agricultural look that fits Wealden land.',
  category: 'Buying Guide',
  publishDate: '2026-07-02',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-wooden-iroko-closeup-kent-weald-countryside.png',
  draft: true,
  excerpt: 'On a working farm drive or a paddock entrance the ornate estate gate looks wrong. A five-bar or estate field gate fits the agricultural setting and the vehicles that actually use it. Here is how to choose one for rural Kent land.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'Not every rural Kent entrance wants an ornamental gate. On a working farm drive, an orchard track near Faversham, a smallholding on the Marsh or a paddock entrance in the Weald, an ornate spear-topped estate gate looks out of place and gets in the way of the vehicles that actually use the opening. The gate that belongs on agricultural and semi-rural land is the field gate: the familiar five-bar in timber or metal, or its heavier estate-gate cousin, sized and hung for tractors, horseboxes, feed lorries and trailers rather than for show. Choosing one well is a practical exercise in matching the gate to the land, the traffic and the local vernacular rather than to a brochure of decorative ironwork.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The appeal is that a well-made field gate reads as though it has always belonged to the place, which matters a great deal on the timber-frame farmhouses, oast conversions and orchard holdings that make up so much of rural Kent. It also tends to be a more honest fit for the budget and the wear a farm entrance actually takes.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'When a field gate is the right call' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A five-bar or estate field gate suits any entrance where the setting is agricultural or genuinely rural and the traffic is working traffic. If tractors, trailers, a horsebox or delivery lorries use the drive, the wide single-leaf or double field gate gives the clear opening they need without the intermediate posts or the delicate detailing that decorative gates carry. It also sits comfortably against grazing land, orchards and paddocks in a way an ornate gate never does, and on protected land it is often the more acceptable design too, because it echoes the traditional agricultural character that planning officers in the [Kent Downs and High Weald National Landscapes](/blog/planning-permission-driveway-gates-kent/) want to see preserved. The [Kent Downs landscape design handbook](https://kentdowns.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2018/04/Landscape-Design-Handbook.pdf) is explicit that elaborate suburban-style gateways read as out of place in the countryside, which is precisely the pitfall a simple field gate avoids. Where the entrance is to a period farmhouse or barn conversion, the field gate is frequently the right aesthetic answer as well, sitting alongside the wider thinking on gates for [older houses and period properties](/blog/driveway-gate-ideas-for-older-houses-and-period-properties/).' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Timber field gates: the traditional Kent look' },
    { type: 'image', src: '/images/gates/gate-wooden-painted-cream-kentish-countryside.png', alt: 'A traditional timber five-bar field gate on rural Kent farmland' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The classic agricultural gate is the timber five-bar, traditionally softwood or, for longer life, a durable hardwood such as oak or sweet chestnut, the latter a genuinely local Kentish coppice timber. Its diagonal brace is not decoration; it stops the gate dropping at the latch end under its own weight, and it should always run from the bottom of the hinge side up towards the latch. A timber field gate weathers to silver-grey, blends into hedgerow and orchard, and can be made to the wider widths a farm needs. The trade-off is that timber wants maintenance and a heavy field gate is a real load on its hinges and post, so the hanging post has to be substantial and well set. For a gate that will be automated, the weight and movement of timber need to be allowed for in the motor choice, which is one reason the field gate should be planned as a whole rather than bought off a stack and mechanised as an afterthought.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Metal field and estate gates' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Galvanised steel field gates are the workhorse of British farms for good reason: they are strong, take hard use, and a hot-dip galvanised finish shrugs off weather with little upkeep. For a smarter agricultural look, the estate gate, a metal gate with closely spaced vertical bars and a gentle curved or humped top rail, gives a more finished appearance while keeping the honest, unfussy character that suits rural land, and it can be powder-coated in estate colours such as black or heritage green. Metal also holds a wide single leaf better than timber over time, which counts on the very wide openings a working entrance sometimes needs. On plots near the East Kent coast the same salt considerations apply as to any metal gate, so a farm entrance within reach of the sea should be galvanised and coated to a coastal standard rather than left as plain steel.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Width, hanging and getting the opening right' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The commonest mistake on a farm gate is making it too narrow. Agricultural vehicles, trailers and horseboxes need real clearance, and a wide field gate as a single leaf swings through a large arc that requires room to open, while a pair of gates or a sliding arrangement can suit a tighter or sloping entrance. Working out the true opening you need, allowing for the widest vehicle and the turning angle off the lane, is the same exercise set out for [measuring a driveway gate opening](/blog/how-wide-should-driveway-gates-be-measuring-guide/), and it is worth doing properly before anything is ordered because a field gate is expensive to get wrong. British field gates are made to recognised sizes, and the dimensions and the way a gate should be hung and braced are covered by the relevant British Standard, with a useful plain-language explanation available from the [British Standards Institution](https://www.bsigroup.com/en-GB/standards/). Match the hanging and latch posts to the gate weight, and set them deep and solid, because a heavy field gate that is under-posted will sag and bind within a season.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'How the gate opens is a separate decision from what it is made of, and it matters more on a farm than on a suburban drive. A single wide leaf is the traditional field-gate arrangement and gives the cleanest opening, but it needs a large clear arc to swing through and a long, strong hanging post to carry the weight, so it suits a flat entrance with room to spare on the inside. A pair of gates halves the swing of each leaf and the load on each post, which helps on a heavy or very wide opening, at the cost of a meeting point in the middle that has to be detailed and secured. Where the lane is tight, the ground slopes, or there simply is not room to swing a leaf, a sliding or tracked arrangement can work better, running the gate along the boundary rather than out into the approach. On rough or uneven farm ground a cantilever slide that needs no ground track avoids the problem of a bottom rail fouling on mud, ruts and frost, which is a common frustration with tracked gates on agricultural surfaces.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Automating a field gate on a farm drive' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A field gate can be automated, and on a busy farm entrance that saves getting out in the rain for every delivery and every trip out. The considerations are the ones that apply to any [automated gate system](/services/automated-gate-systems/), scaled for the weight and the width of an agricultural gate: a motor sized for a heavy, wide leaf, a control setup that copes with frequent use, and a power supply that reaches an entrance often set well back from the mains. On long rural drives the way that gate is powered becomes its own decision, and the trade-offs between solar, trenched mains and battery backup are exactly those set out for [powering a gate on a long rural Kent farm drive](/blog/off-grid-power-for-gates-on-rural-kent-farm-drives/). If you want a five-bar or estate field gate for a farm, paddock or orchard entrance in rural Kent, tell us the vehicles that use it and how far the gate sits from any power, and use the form on this page to be matched with local installers who fit agricultural gates rather than only suburban ones.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};


const article28: BlogArticle = {
  slug: 'protecting-a-rural-kent-gate-against-farm-and-plant-theft',
  title: 'Protecting a Rural Kent Gate Against Farm and Plant Theft',
  metaTitle: 'Rural Kent Gate Security: Farm and Plant Theft | Guide',
  metaDescription: 'How a driveway gate defends a rural Kent farm or smallholding against quad, plant and trailer theft. Anti-lift hinges, field-gate locking and gate design for isolated land.',
  category: 'Security',
  publishDate: '2026-07-02',
  featuredImage: '/images/gates/gate-wrought-iron-open-manor-brick-pillars.png',
  draft: true,
  excerpt: 'Rural crime hits Kent hard, and a farm gate at the end of a long unlit drive is both the first line of defence and, done badly, an easy weak point. Here is how to design and secure a gate against quad, plant and trailer theft.',
  content: [
    { type: 'p', text: 'Rural crime is a real and rising pressure on Kent farms and smallholdings, not a background worry. Organised gangs target agricultural land for the things that are portable, valuable and quick to move: quad bikes and all-terrain vehicles, GPS units off tractors, tools, trailers and plant. Quad and ATV theft alone rose sharply again in the most recent figures, and the equipment stolen is often essential to a working business rather than merely expensive to replace. A driveway or yard gate is the first physical line of defence on isolated land, but only if it is designed and fitted to resist a determined thief, because a gate that can be lifted off its hinges, cut through or simply driven at is worse than no gate at all: it gives false confidence. The scale of the problem is set out each year in the [NFU Mutual rural crime report](https://www.nfumutual.co.uk/farming/rural-crime), which makes uncomfortable reading for anyone farming in the county.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The good news is that a well-specified gate genuinely raises the effort and the time a theft takes, and time and effort are what opportunist and organised thieves alike want to avoid. The details that make the difference are not exotic; they are about how the gate is hung, how it locks, and how it fits into the wider security of the holding.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'The weak point: hinges and how a gate is lifted' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'The most common way a swing gate is defeated is not by cutting the lock but by lifting the gate straight off its hinges. A standard hook-and-band or pin hinge lets a gate be lifted vertically once it is unlatched, and a thief with a strong back or a strap can have a leaf off in seconds. The answer is anti-lift hinges, where the hinge is reversed, capped or fitted with a locking collar or a bottom hinge that faces the opposite way to the top, so the gate cannot be raised off its pins while it is hung. On a heavy agricultural gate this is cheap to specify at installation and awkward to retrofit, which is a good reason to raise it when the gate is being planned. The same principle applies to the pedestrian or field gate alongside, and it feeds into the wider [home and property security thinking for a Kent gate](/blog/driveway-gates-home-security-kent/), where the gate is only as strong as its weakest fitting.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Locking a field or yard gate properly' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A gate is only as secure as the way it is held shut. On a manually operated field or yard gate the standard farm approach is a heavy locking bar or a padlock and hardened chain rated to resist bolt croppers, fitted so the shackle is shrouded and hard to get a cutter onto. Where two leaves meet, a drop bolt into a ground socket stops the closed edges being forced apart. On an automated gate the motor itself resists being pushed open, but the specification should still include a manual lock for when the gate is left for long periods, because a determined thief will test whether an unpowered gate can be forced. Kent Police publish practical, county-specific advice on securing rural property and marking equipment through their [rural crime prevention guidance](https://www.kent.police.uk/advice/advice-and-information/rc/rural-crime/rural-crime-prevention/), and their consistent message is that layered, visible security deters more than any single measure.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'Gate design for an isolated entrance' },
    { type: 'image', src: '/images/gates/gate-wrought-iron-detail-spear-finials-closeup.png', alt: 'Close detail of a robust metal driveway gate suited to a rural Kent property' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'How the gate is built matters as much as how it locks. A gate at the end of a long, unlit drive should be solid enough that it cannot be quickly cut or bent, hung on posts set deep and in concrete so it cannot be levered out of the ground, and positioned so it cannot simply be driven around across a soft verge, which often means running stout fencing or a ditch to either side. Height and visibility both play a part: a gate that is seen, and that is clearly hard work to beat, is one a thief moves past. Automatic closing is a quiet but important defence on a farm entrance, because the most common failure is a gate left open by accident after a delivery, and a gate that closes itself behind every vehicle removes that lapse. Where power reaches the entrance, a camera or an intercom at the gate adds a real deterrent and a record, and on the most exposed coastal plots the salt that attacks a gate also attacks the locks, hinges and electronics that keep it secure, so the security hardware needs to be as weather-resistant as the gate itself.' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'Materials play into this as well. A gate that a thief has to work at is one built from something that resists a battery grinder or a crowbar for long enough to matter, which on a farm usually means solid galvanised steel or a heavy hardwood rather than a thin decorative panel. Glazed or infilled sections that can be kicked through are a weakness on a security gate, and low-set horizontal rails that give a foothold make an easy gate to climb, so a security-minded field or estate gate tends to be tall, close-barred and solid at the bottom. None of this means a fortress: a gate that looks proportionate to a farmhouse can still be quietly robust, and the aim is to raise the effort and the noise a break-in takes rather than to build something that advertises what is behind it, which can attract as much attention as it deters.' },
    { type: 'h2', text: 'The gate as one layer of a secure holding' },
    { type: 'p', text: 'A gate does not secure a farm on its own, and the mistake is to treat it as if it does. It works as the outer layer of a scheme that also marks and immobilises the machines thieves want, keeps quad bikes and tools locked away rather than left in an open barn, lights the approach, and joins a local rural watch so a suspicious vehicle in the lane is reported rather than ignored. The gate buys time and raises effort at the boundary; everything behind it does the rest. Keeping the gate itself working reliably is part of that, because a gate that has seized or lost its automatic close is a gap in the line, which is why routine [gate repair and maintenance](/services/gate-repair-and-maintenance/) belongs on the same security checklist as marking the plant. If you farm or keep a smallholding in rural Kent and want a gate specified to resist theft rather than just to look the part, tell us what the holding is exposed to and how remote the entrance is, and use the form on this page to be matched with local installers who fit anti-lift hinges, proper locking and security-minded automation.' },
    { type: 'cta' },
  ],
};


